{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GNPkQQtnaLfHzPdVa4dJhG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-12 16:00 +07\n2\t# Task: write the SIMPLE discovery-order numbering (round 4, fresh Architect session)\n3\t\n4\tYou are claude-fable-5-1, a NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering`, branch `numbering-note`. Edit ONLY `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, the prototype under `collab/numbering-probe`, and your report `collab/1792220000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise3.report.md`. No git add/commit. Vocabulary exactly as UCF section 7.5.3 (cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell).\n5\t\n6\tContext. The owner chose discovery-order numbering to replace phase 6a's refinement+search because it is simple and matches the de Bruijn style of the register and stack VMs. Your round-3 note (980 lines, variants D5 and D6) was reviewed by codex (`collab/1792210000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`, CHANGES_REQUESTED: 2 HIGH, 3 MEDIUM, and \"D6 is not materially simpler than 6a\"). The owner then gave this design direction (follow it):\n7\t\n8\t1. GUEST PROGRAMS ARE UNRESTRICTED. Sets and maps may contain cursor-refs and cursor-refs may be map keys. Do NOT forbid anything in guest code.\n9\t2. TRANSFORM unordered containers into ordered sequences in the carrier before numbering: a map becomes a vector of `[key value]` tuples; a set becomes a vector of its members. Their ORDER is a deterministic function of the structure (sort by an encoding of the member/key that spells already-numbered cursor cells by number and not-yet-numbered ones by body plus local first-occurrence index, as in your D4 repair), never of host enumeration or original ids.\n10\t3. After that transformation EVERYTHING is an ordered position, and numbering is plain discovery order (de Bruijn style): a cursor cell gets the next number the first time the fixed walk meets it. For `[P Q P]` this gives `[1 2 1]` whatever the bodies.\n11\t4. TIES: members that remain tied after the sort (equal encodings). If swapping the tied members provably leaves the whole state unchanged (interchangeable), any order is fine. If they are tied but NOT interchangeable (the path example: `#{[a b] [b c]}` with equal bodies, where numbering a first versus b first gives different bytes) the carrier REFUSES at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, decode translates only that to `:yin.k/undecodable`, with the install-child path fix of round 3. Specify the cheapest SOUND test for \"interchangeable\" (sound means: it may refuse a state that is in fact interchangeable, but it must never accept one that is not; say precisely what it refuses). D6 residue search is NOT part of the base; keep it in one short appendix \"optional extension, not adopted\", with its proof sketch and your measurements, nothing more.\n12\t\n13\tTargets. (a) The normative part of the note, what an implementer needs, must be SHORT: aim for at most 300 lines, readable top to bottom: the rule, the transformation, the walk, the tie test, the refusal, the decode translation, the budgets (`:work` bytes budget with `:resource-limit`, keep rank spelling), the `:cursor-cells` and `:depth` gates that 6a enforces (codex MEDIUM: replacement must keep them), the totality assertion and the required rewrite of the large-literal test (codex MEDIUM: base declares an unreachable cell). Move everything else (D5/D6 variants, long proofs, measurements) to appendices after the normative part, and mark them non-normative. (b) Exactly one short proof of canonicality for the base design, covering the sort, the walk and the tie test, with a decreasing measure for termination (codex HIGH 2): there is no recursion in the base, so say why. (c) Fix the coverage paragraph (codex MEDIUM: mixed-body components and single anchors); state what is refused with qualified examples, and the measured generator rate. (d) Keep every counterexample as a fixture. (e) Update the \"Replacing 6a\" section (what rows.cljc/rows_test.cljc delete, rewrite, keep, with `srvm-phase6a` line numbers).\n14\tPrototype: make the probe implement exactly the base design (D6 off, behind a flag only if you keep the appendix), rerun the random renaming/permutation search (nested sets, sets of pairs, map keys that are cursor-refs, tied elements, parked states, K frames, install children), and report counts: numbered, refused (split into interchangeable-but-refused and truly non-interchangeable), invariance failures (must be 0), decode round trips. Also measure how many random states a more permissive tie test would have accepted if any, to justify the one you chose.\n15\tForeground only; do not background anything. The report must begin with a plain-language summary for the owner in under 25 lines (no jargon beyond the agreed vocabulary): what the rule is, an example, what is refused and why, and how it compares in size and difficulty with phase 6a's refinement+search (give honest line counts). End with READY or NOT READY for an implementation brief and the remaining risks.\n16\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c7b287b-b5f3-49ed-8e51-732a8bb9b9de","uuid":"d818ab29-89b7-41f8-b33d-6585aa66f90b","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:47:23.196Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/1792220000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise3.prompt.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-12 16:00 +07\n# Task: write the SIMPLE discovery-order numbering (round 4, fresh Architect session)\n\nYou are claude-fable-5-1, a NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering`, branch `numbering-note`. Edit ONLY `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, the prototype under `collab/numbering-probe`, and your report `collab/1792220000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise3.report.md`. No git