le\" (sound means: it may refuse a state that is in fact interchangeable, but it must never accept one that is not; say precisely what it refuses). D6 residue search is NOT part of the base; keep it in one short appendix \"optional extension, not adopted\", with its proof sketch and your measurements, nothing more.\n\nTargets. (a) The normative part of the note, what an implementer needs, must be SHORT: aim for at most 300 lines, readable top to bottom: the rule, the transformation, the walk, the tie test, the refusal, the decode translation, the budgets (`:work` bytes budget with `:resource-limit`, keep rank spelling), the `:cursor-cells` and `:depth` gates that 6a enforces (codex MEDIUM: replacement must keep them), the totality assertion and the required rewrite of the large-literal test (codex MEDIUM: base declares an unreachable cell). Move everything else (D5/D6 variants, long proofs, measurements) to appendices after the normative part, and mark them non-normative. (b) Exactly one short proof of canonicality for the base design, covering the sort, the walk and the tie test, with a decreasing measure for termination (codex HIGH 2): there is no recursion in the base, so say why. (c) Fix the coverage paragraph (codex MEDIUM: mixed-body components and single anchors); state what is refused with qualified examples, and the measured generator rate. (d) Keep every counterexample as a fixture. (e) Update the \"Replacing 6a\" section (what rows.cljc/rows_test.cljc delete, rewrite, keep, with `srvm-phase6a` line numbers).\nPrototype: make the probe implement exactly the base design (D6 off, behind a flag only if you keep the appendix), rerun the random renaming/permutation search (nested sets, sets of pairs, map keys that are cursor-refs, tied elements, parked states, K frames, install children), and report counts: numbered, refused (split into interchangeable-but-refused and truly non-interchangeable), invariance failures (must be 0), decode round trips. Also measure how many random states a more permissive tie test would have accepted if any, to justify the one you chose.\nForeground only; do not background anything. The report must begin with a plain-language summary for the owner in under 25 lines (no jargon beyond the agreed vocabulary): what the rule is, an example, what is refused and why, and how it compares in size and difficulty with phase 6a's refinement+search (give honest line counts). End with READY or NOT READY for an implementation brief and the remaining risks.\n","numLines":16,"startLine":1,"totalLines":16}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GNPkQQtnaLfHzPdVa4dJhG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
