{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQ2mN86soJ8Mvw3NhAu9Zo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns probe.discovery\n2\t  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (note D1-D6, round 3 of\n3\t   2026-10-12) over the 6a abstract task representation (yin.vm.ucf.rows).\n4\t   Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/.\n5\t\n6\t   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n7\t   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n8\t\n9\t   Shape of the procedure (one algorithm, three uses):\n10\t   - a RUN traverses an item (D1), numbers cursor cells on first meeting (D2),\n11\t     keys every :set / :map element by a speculative local run and defers equal\n12\t     keys to the run's worklist (D1), re-examines the worklist to a fixpoint\n13\t     (D3), groups the surviving tied elements into components by shared\n14\t     unnumbered cursor cells, encodes every component by a local run over its\n15\t     component item and adopts the least encoding's components (D4). A\n16\t     component holding two elements of one group is refused (D5) or, with\n17\t     :residue-search, resolved by trials (D6): one local run per element of\n18\t     the duplicated group with that element entered first, the least\n19\t     spelling adopted.\n20\t   - the root run assigns [:n k], k = 1, 2, ...; a LOCAL run assigns [:u j r]\n21\t     (j from the enclosing run's local counter, r the body rank) and its\n22\t     spelling is the key. A KEY run is speculative: it never refuses, and its\n23\t     residue (ties or conflicts) is spelled [:tie K ...] in the key. A\n24\t     COMPONENT run (and a D6 trial) must be complete, else D5.\n25\t   - :work is charged per position visited or spelled and per literal byte\n26\t     copied into a spelling; :search per D6 trial; exceeding either refuses\n27\t     :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\"\n28\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n29\t            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n30\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n31\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n32\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n33\t\n34\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n35\t\n36\t(def synthetic-table\n37\t  \"Rows that exist only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an ordered pair,\n38\t   and a component as the ordered list of its (group-id item) pairs, where the\n39\t   item is the group's one element or a synthetic :set of its elements.\"\n40\t  {:entry [:V :V] :component [:pairs :D :V]})\n41\t\n42\t(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n43\t  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n44\t    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n45\t          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n46\t          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n47\t          :else schema)))\n48\t\n49\t(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n50\t(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n51\t(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n52\t\n53\t(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n54\t\n55\t(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n56\t  (loop [i 0]\n57\t    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n58\t          (= i (count b)) 1\n59\t          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n60\t          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n61\t\n62\t(defn by-key-bytes [f xs] (sort #(compare-bytes (f %1) (f %2)) xs))\n63\t\n64\t(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n65\t\n66\t(defn elem-item\n67\t  \"The item an element is entered and keyed as: a :set element is its V; a\n68\t   :map entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v], so that the\n69\t   alias pattern across key and value is part of the key.\"\n70\t  [kind e]\n71\t  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n72\t\n73\t(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n74\t\n75\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n76\t\n77\t(def default-budget\n78\t  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes; :search now counts D6 trials.\"\n79\t  (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes))\n80\t\n81\t(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n82\t  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n83\t    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category))\n84\t      (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n85\t                      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]})))))\n86\t\n87\t(defn- literal-bytes [x] (count (cbor/encode x)))\n88\t\n89\t(defn item-cells\n90\t  \"Cursor cells occurring in slot (d x); census and install children excluded.\n91\t   Memoized per procedure on the slot value.\"\n92\t  [ctx d x]\n93\t  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\n94\t      (let [acc (atom #{})]\n95\t        (letfn [(slot [d x]\n96\t                  (cond (ref? d) (node x)\n97\t                        (refs? d) (run! node x)\n98\t                        (= :C d) (swap! acc conj x)\n99\t                        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n100\t                                         :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second x))\n101\t                                         :row (node (second x))\n102\t                                         nil))\n103\t                        :else nil))\n104\t                (node [n]\n105\t                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n106\t                    (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (child-task? ctx n))\n107\t                      (dorun (map slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n108\t          (slot d x)\n109\t          (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\n110\t          @acc))))\n111\t\n112\t(defn body-label [body]\n113\t  (let [[stream position] (:slots body)]\n114\t    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n115\t\n116\t(defn body-ranks\n117\t  \"Cursor cell -> rank of its body label among the census's distinct body\n118\t   labels in bytewise order. Rank order equals label order, so spelling the\n119\t   rank instead of the label changes no comparison and no numbering; it keeps\n120\t   key bytes independent of the descriptor size.\"\n121\t  [bodies]\n122\t  (let [labels (into {} (map (fn [[c b]] [c (body-label b)])) bodies)\n123\t        ranks (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i l] [l i]))\n124\t                    (by-key-bytes bytes-key (distinct (vals labels))))]\n125\t    (into {} (map (fn [[c l]] [c (get ranks l)])) labels)))\n126\t\n127\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\n128\t\n129\t(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\n130\t\n131\t(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next first-item]\n132\t  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next) :first (atom first-item)\n133\t   :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\n134\t\n135\t(defn- meet!\n136\t  \"D2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\"\n137\t  [run c]\n138\t  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\n139\t    (let [k @(:next run)]\n140\t      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\n141\t      (swap! (:next run) inc)\n142\t      true)))\n143\t\n144\t(declare enter-node! enter-slot! unordered! local-run resolve! spell-slot spell-node)\n145\t\n146\t(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\n147\t  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n148\t  (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\n149\t        (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\n150\t        (= :C d) (meet! run x)\n151\t        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n152\t                         :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\n153\t                         :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\n154\t                         nil))\n155\t        :else nil))\n156\t\n157\t(defn- enter-node!\n158\t  \"D1. Canonical traversal: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in\n159\t   order; keyed structural rows in the Jing byte order of the key; :window in\n160\t   ascending register order; installs and module stores by name; :set and\n161\t   :map by local keys with ties deferred; census and children not entered.\"\n162\t  [run n path]\n163\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n164\t    (cond\n165\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n166\t      (child-task? ctx n) nil\n167\t      (= :task kind)\n168\t      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n169\t        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n170\t          (doseq [child (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first (:slots %))) x)]\n171\t            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\n172\t          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\n173\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n174\t      (doseq [[k v] (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %)) (partition 2 slots))]\n175\t        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\n176\t      (= :window kind)\n177\t      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\n178\t      (= :set kind) (unordered! run :set (vec slots) path)\n179\t      (= :map kind) (unordered! run :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\n180\t      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n181\t\n182\t(defn- local-key [run item]\n183\t  (let [r (local-run run item nil)] [(bytes-key (:spell r)) (:spell r)]))\n184\t\n185\t(defn- unordered!\n186\t  \"D1 for an unordered container: key every element by a speculative local\n187\t   run under the numbering at this moment; enter singletons in key order;\n188\t   defer equal runs as one tie group (deferral order = canonical order). A D6\n189\t   trial run enters its forced element first, when its container is keyed.\"\n190\t  [run kind elems path]\n191\t  (let [f @(:first run)\n192\t        forced (when f (first (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (= f (elem-item kind e)) i)) elems)))]\n193\t    (when forced\n194\t      (reset! (:first run) nil)\n195\t      (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind (nth elems forced)) (conj path forced)))\n196\t    (let [keyed (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (not= i forced) [(local-key run (elem-item kind e)) e i])) elems))\n197\t          groups (partition-by (comp first first) (sort #(compare-bytes (first (first %1)) (first (first %2))) keyed))]\n198\t      (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n199\t        (println \"  [sort]\" path \"num\" @(:num run))\n200\t        (doseq [g groups] (println \"    \" (if (= 1 (count g)) \"enter\" \"DEFER\") (mapv (fn [[[_ sp] e _]] [e sp]) g))))\n201\t      (doseq [g groups]\n202\t        (if (= 1 (count g))\n203\t          (let [[_ e i] (first g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))\n204\t          (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :kind kind :path path\n205\t                                     :key (second (first (first g))) :elems (mapv second g)}))))))\n206\t\n207\t(defn- fixpoint!\n208\t  \"D3. Re-examine every deferred group in deferral order under the current\n209\t   numbering; repeat while a round numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\"\n210\t  [run]\n211\t  (loop []\n212\t    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\n213\t      (reset! (:groups run) [])\n214\t      (doseq [{:keys [kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run kind elems path))\n215\t      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\n216\t\n217\t(defn- components\n218\t  \"D4. Tied elements of all surviving groups, linked by shared unnumbered\n219\t   cursor cells (union-find). A component is :conflict when it holds two\n220\t   elements of one group; :dup-group is the least such group.\"\n221\t  [run]\n222\t  (let [num @(:num run) ctx (:ctx run)\n223\t        by-id (into {} (map (juxt :id identity)) @(:groups run))\n224\t        elems (vec (for [g @(:groups run) [i e] (map-indexed vector (:elems g))]\n225\t                     {:group (:id g) :kind (:kind g) :elem e :idx i\n226\t                      :cells (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells ctx :V (elem-item (:kind g) e))))}))\n227\t        parent (atom (vec (range (count elems))))\n228\t        find (fn find [i] (let [p (@parent i)] (if (= p i) i (let [r (find p)] (swap! parent assoc i r) r))))\n229\t        union (fn [i j] (let [ri (find i) rj (find j)] (when (not= ri rj) (swap! parent assoc ri rj))))\n230\t        by-cell (reduce (fn [m [i el]] (reduce (fn [m c] (update m c (fnil conj []) i)) m (:cells el)))\n231\t                        {} (map-indexed vector elems))]\n232\t    (doseq [[_ is] by-cell] (doseq [j (rest is)] (union (first is) j)))\n233\t    (->> (group-by find (range (count elems)))\n234\t         (sort-by first)\n235\t         (mapv (fn [[_ is]]\n236\t                 (let [members (vec (sort-by (juxt :group :idx) (map elems is)))\n237\t                       dup (first (sort (keep (fn [[g c]] (when (> c 1) g)) (frequencies (map :group members)))))]\n238\t                   {:members members\n239\t                    :conflict (some? dup)\n240\t                    :dup-group dup\n241\t                    :path (:path (get by-id (or dup (:group (first members)))))\n242\t                    :cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat :cells members))))}))))))\n243\t\n244\t(defn- component-item\n245\t  \"[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …] in group order; iᵢ is the group's one element\n246\t   in the component, or a synthetic :set of its elements when there are\n247\t   several (the D6 case).\"\n248\t  [comp]\n249\t  [:row {:kind :component\n250\t         :slots (vec (mapcat (fn [ms]\n251\t                               [(:group (first ms))\n252\t                                (if (= 1 (count ms))\n253\t                                  (elem-item (:kind (first ms)) (:elem (first ms)))\n254\t                                  [:row {:kind :set :slots (mapv #(elem-item (:kind %) (:elem %)) ms)}])])\n255\t                             (partition-by :group (:members comp))))}])\n256\t\n257\t(defn- component-encoding\n258\t  \"D4 for a certified component: a local run over its item, required to be\n259\t   complete. D5 for a conflicting component: refuse. D6 (:residue-search): one\n260\t   trial per element of the duplicated group, that element entered first; the\n261\t   least complete spelling is the encoding. :permissive (diagnostic only):\n262\t   a single trial with the host-first element.\"\n263\t  [run comp]\n264\t  (let [ctx (:ctx run) item (component-item comp)]\n265\t    (cond\n266\t      (not (:conflict comp)) (local-run run item nil)\n267\t      (:permissive ctx)\n268\t      (let [m (first (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp)))]\n269\t        (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m))))\n270\t      (:residue-search ctx)\n271\t      (let [es (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp))\n272\t            trials (mapv (fn [m] (spend! ctx :search 1) (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m)))) es)]\n273\t        (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n274\t          (doseq [t trials] (println \"  [trial]\" (:complete? t) (:spell t))))\n275\t        (or (first (remove :complete? trials))\n276\t            (first (sort-by #(bytes-key (:spell %)) compare-bytes trials))))\n277\t      :else {:complete? false\n278\t             :refused {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :path (:path comp) :cursor-cells (:cells comp)}})))\n279\t\n280\t(defn- adopt!\n281\t  \"D4. Enter a component by adopting its local run: its fresh cursor cells\n282\t   receive the enclosing run's next numbers in local-index order.\"\n283\t  [run comp]\n284\t  (let [fresh (->> (:num (:ce comp))\n285\t                   (remove (fn [[c _]] (contains? @(:num run) c)))\n286\t                   (sort-by (comp second val)))]\n287\t    (doseq [[c _] fresh] (meet! run c))))\n288\t\n289\t(defn- remove-elements! [run comps]\n290\t  (let [gone (set (for [c comps m (:members c)] [(:group m) (:idx m)]))]\n291\t    (swap! (:groups run)\n292\t           (fn [gs] (vec (keep (fn [g]\n293\t                                 (let [es (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (gone [(:id g) i]) e)) (:elems g)))]\n294\t                                   (when (seq es) (assoc g :elems es))))\n295\t                               gs))))))\n296\t\n297\t(defn- resolve!\n298\t  \"D3-D6 to completion. Leaves :refused set (D5) with the worklist as of the\n299\t   last fixpoint, or the worklist empty.\"\n300\t  [run]\n301\t  (loop []\n302\t    (fixpoint! run)\n303\t    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n304\t      (println \"  [resolve] num\" @(:num run) \"groups\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (mapv (fn [e] (if (= :cursor-cell (first e)) (second e) e)) (:elems g))]) @(:groups run))))\n305\t    (when (seq @(:groups run))\n306\t      (let [comps (mapv #(assoc % :ce (component-encoding run %)) (components run))\n307\t            bad (seq (remove #(:complete? (:ce %)) comps))]\n308\t        (if bad\n309\t          ;; The verdict is invariant; the diagnostic names every conflicting\n310\t          ;; component's cells (sorted) and the least conflicting group's path,\n311\t          ;; so it does not depend on host order either.\n312\t          (reset! (:refused run) {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n313\t                                  :path (:path (:refused (:ce (first (sort-by #(or (:dup-group %) Long/MAX_VALUE) bad)))))\n314\t                                  :cursor-cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat #(:cursor-cells (:refused (:ce %))) bad))))})\n315\t          (let [sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %1))) (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %2)))) comps)\n316\t                least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce (first sorted))))\n317\t                least-run (take-while #(= least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %)))) sorted)]\n318\t            (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n319\t              (doseq [c sorted] (println \"  [component]\" (mapv (fn [m] [(:group m) (:cells m)]) (:members c)) \"CE\" (:spell (:ce c))))\n320\t              (println \"  [adopt]\" (count least-run)))\n321\t            (doseq [c least-run] (adopt! run c))\n322\t            (remove-elements! run least-run)\n323\t            (recur)))))))\n324\t\n325\t(defn- local-run\n326\t  \"A nested run on one item from the enclosing run's numbering, tag :u. Its\n327\t   result is a key: the spelling under its final numbering, surviving ties\n328\t   spelled [:tie K ...]. `first-item`, when given (D6 trial), is entered\n329\t   before its siblings. Memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the item's\n330\t   cursor cells, local counter, first-item).\"\n331\t  [run item first-item]\n332\t  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\n333\t        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\n334\t        num @(:num run)\n335\t        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\n336\t        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start first-item]]\n337\t    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\n338\t        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start first-item)]\n339\t          (enter-slot! sub :V item [])\n340\t          (resolve! sub)\n341\t          (let [spell (spell-slot sub :V item [])\n342\t                r {:spell spell\n343\t                   ;; Restricted to the item's own cursor cells: the result is a\n344\t                   ;; function of the memo key, never of the enclosing context.\n345\t                   :num (select-keys @(:num sub) cells)\n346\t                   :complete? (and (nil? @(:refused sub)) (empty? @(:groups sub)))\n347\t                   :refused @(:refused sub)}]\n348\t            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :key-bytes (fnil + 0) (count (bytes-key spell)))\n349\t            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :local-runs (fnil + 0) 1)\n350\t            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\n351\t            r)))))\n352\t\n353\t;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\n354\t\n355\t(defn- spell-cell [run c]\n356\t  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c) ctx (:ctx run)]\n357\t    (case t\n358\t      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\n359\t      :u [:cursor-cell [:u k (if (= :label (:spell-body ctx))\n360\t                               (body-label (get (:bodies ctx) c))\n361\t                               (get (:ranks ctx) c))]]\n362\t      (throw (ex-info \"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\n363\t\n364\t(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\n365\t  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n366\t  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n367\t        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n368\t        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n369\t        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n370\t                   (case (first x)\n371\t                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\n372\t                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\n373\t                     (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes (second x))) x)))\n374\t        :else (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes x)) x)))\n375\t\n376\t(defn- spell-node\n377\t  \"The content encoding CE of a row under the run's final numbering.\"\n378\t  [run n path]\n379\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n380\t    (cond\n381\t      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n382\t      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\n383\t      (= :task kind)\n384\t      (into [kind]\n385\t            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\n386\t                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n387\t                             (vec (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (nth % 1))\n388\t                                                (map #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x)))\n389\t                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\n390\t                         (map vector ds slots)))\n391\t      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n392\t      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %))\n393\t                                                  (map (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\n394\t                                                       (partition 2 slots)))))\n395\t      (= :window kind)\n396\t      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n397\t      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\n398\t      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n399\t            tied (filter #(= path (:path %)) @(:groups run))\n400\t            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\n401\t            plain (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))) elems)\n402\t            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\n403\t        (into [kind] (by-key-bytes bytes-key (concat plain ties))))\n404\t      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n405\t\n406\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\n407\t\n408\t(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts*\n409\t  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install children):\n410\t   :residue-search (D6), :permissive (diagnostic), :spell-body :label (the\n411\t   round-2 spelling, for the byte measurement), :budget.\"\n412\t  {})\n413\t\n414\t(defn discovery-numbering\n415\t  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n416\t   Throws :resource-limit when the budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n417\t   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n418\t  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n419\t  ([task opts]\n420\t   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n421\t         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n422\t         ctx {:task task\n423\t              :bodies bodies\n424\t              :ranks (body-ranks bodies)\n425\t              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n426\t              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n427\t              :counter (atom {})\n428\t              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n429\t              :residue-search (:residue-search opts)\n430\t              :spell-body (:spell-body opts)}\n431\t         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1 nil)]\n432\t     (enter-node! run task [])\n433\t     (resolve! run)\n434\t     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n435\t       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n436\t       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n437\t         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n438\t         (when (not= numbered declared)\n439\t           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (remove numbered declared))\n440\t                                                      :extra (vec (remove declared numbered))})))\n441\t         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n442\t          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n443\t\n444\t;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n445\t;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n446\t;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n447\t\n448\t(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n449\t(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n450\t(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n451\t\n452\t(declare emit-node*)\n453\t\n454\t(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\n455\t  (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n456\t                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n457\t                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)})))\n458\t\n459\t(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows path]\n460\t  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n461\t    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n462\t  (cond\n463\t    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows path)\n464\t    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] [j n]]\n465\t                        (let [p (conj path (if (= :install (:kind n)) (first (:slots n)) j))\n466\t                              [id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs p)]\n467\t                          [(conj out id) rs']))\n468\t                      [[] rows] (map-indexed vector x))\n469\t    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n470\t    (v? d) (case (first x)\n471\t             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows path)] [[:row id] rs])\n472\t             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n473\t             [x rows])\n474\t    :else [x rows]))\n475\t\n476\t(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows path]\n477\t  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n478\t        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [i d x]]\n479\t                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n480\t                                 ;; An install child: its own scope, numbered by the same procedure.\n481\t                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x)\n482\t                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) path))\n483\t                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {} [])]\n484\t                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n485\t                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs (conj path i))] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n486\t                             [[] rows] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n487\t        slots (if (= kind :task)\n488\t                (-> slots\n489\t                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n490\t                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n491\t                (ordered-slots kind slots))\n492\t        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\n493\t    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\n494\t\n495\t(defn emit*\n496\t  \"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\n497\t   :unresolved-cursor-cells (D5) for the root or for any install child (path\n498\t   [12 <name> …]); a budget refusal propagates as itself.\"\n499\t  [task]\n500\t  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n501\t    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\n502\t    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {} [])]\n503\t      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\n504\t\n505\t(defn decode*\n506\t  \"Receiver side: 6a's decode* with its own numbering check off (it still runs\n507\t   6a's recompute, under 6a's budget), then the whole discovery recomputation\n508\t   (root and install children) under one translation: only a lift-side\n509\t   :yin.k/non-portable of kind :unresolved-cursor-cells becomes\n510\t   :yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells with the path prefixed by the\n511\t   carried root id; anything else (a resource refusal) propagates as itself.\n512\t   In the real codec this check sits at carrier step 6, before\n513\t   validate-frames; here it runs after 6a's decode* returns.\"\n514\t  [wire]\n515\t  (let [task (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false))\n516\t        root (:yin.k/root wire)\n517\t        id (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n518\t                (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n519\t                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n520\t                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d)) (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n521\t                      (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n522\t                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n523\t                      (throw e)))))]\n524\t    (when (not= id root)\n525\t      (throw (ex-info \"noncanonical\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\n526\t                                      :yin.k/path [root]})))\n527\t    task))\n528\t\n529\t(defn error [f]\n530\t  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\n531\t\n532\t(defn do-root\n533\t  \"Root id, {:refused …} for D5, or {:resource-limit path} for a budget.\"\n534\t  [task]\n535\t  (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n536\t       (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n537\t         (let [d (ex-data e)]\n538\t           (case (:yin.k/kind d)\n539\t             :unresolved-cursor-cells {:refused d}\n540\t             :resource-limit {:resource-limit (:yin.k/path d)}\n541\t             (throw e))))))\n542\t\n543\t(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n544\t  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n545\t       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n546\t\n547\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n548\t\n549\t(defn rename-and-shuffle\n550\t  \"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set, :map and the\n551\t   census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers for determinism).\"\n552\t  [n renaming]\n553\t  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n554\t                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n555\t                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n556\t                       v))\n557\t          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\n558\t          (go [n]\n559\t            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n560\t              (case kind\n561\t                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\n562\t                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n563\t                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n564\t                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n565\t                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n566\t                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n567\t                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n568\t                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n569\t                                              :else x))\n570\t                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n571\t    (go n)))\n572\t\n573\t(defn all-census-cells\n574\t  \"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\"\n575\t  [task]\n576\t  (let [acc (atom [])]\n577\t    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\n578\t                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\n579\t                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\n580\t                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\n581\t                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\n582\t      (node task) (vec @acc))))\n583\t\n584\t(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\n585\t  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\n586\t             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\n587\t             (vec a))))\n588\t\n589\t(defn decoded\n590\t  \"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule):\n591\t   sets as sets, maps as maps, cursor cells by identity.\"\n592\t  [v]\n593\t  (case (first v)\n594\t    :lit [:lit (second v)]\n595\t    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\n596\t    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\n597\t           (case (:kind n)\n598\t             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\n599\t             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\n600\t             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\n601\t             [:row n]))))\n602\t\n603\t(defn distinct-decoded [vs] (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\n604\t\n605\t(defn gen-value\n606\t  \"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded. Shapes: literals, cursor cells,\n607\t   vectors, sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs\n608\t   (edges, aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\"\n609\t  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n610\t  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n611\t        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\n612\t        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\n613\t    (case k\n614\t      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n615\t      1 (cc)\n616\t      2 (cc)\n617\t      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n618\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\n619\t      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n620\t          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n621\t      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\n622\t                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n623\t          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n624\t            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n625\t            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\n626\t      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\n627\t              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\n628\t      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\n629\t                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\n630\t                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\n631\t          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\n632\t\n633\t(defn reached [v]\n634\t  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n635\t    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n636\t                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n637\t                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n638\t                     nil))]\n639\t      (w v) @acc)))\n640\t\n641\t(defn gen-task\n642\t  \"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\n643\t   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\"\n644\t  [seed]\n645\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n646\t        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\n647\t        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n648\t        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\n649\t        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n650\t        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n651\t        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n652\t        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n653\t        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n654\t    (if (empty? used)\n655\t      (recur (inc seed))\n656\t      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n657\t        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n658\t\n659\t(defn worked-act []\n660\t  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\n661\t\n662\t(def park-vector [[:closure 0 [] 2] [:halt 0] [:park 0] [:return 0]])\n663\t(def park-address (jing/segment-key park-vector))\n664\t\n665\t(defn frame-task\n666\t  \"A blocked task carrying the worked code plus a park segment. The wait's\n667\t   act holds `wait-values` in its window register 1 and in one K frame's\n668\t   window register 1; `env-value`, when given, is bound to e0 in the free\n669\t   environment and in every environment; the parked state (P 7) is an act\n670\t   at the park site whose K frame holds `parked-value` in register 1.\n671\t   Decodes under 6a's validate-frames.\"\n672\t  [census wait-values env-value parked-value]\n673\t  (let [base (rt/task-node (conj (vec census) [:test/cursor (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]) nil)\n674\t        [seg pc env window k rd] (:slots (worked-act))\n675\t        [_ store-of] (:slots env)\n676\t        bindings (rows/node :bindings (if env-value ['e0 env-value] []))\n677\t        env (rows/node :env [bindings store-of])\n678\t        kframe (fn [v] (rows/node :kframe [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 v]) rd]))\n679\t        [w1 w2] wait-values\n680\t        wait-act (rows/node :act [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 w1]) (rows/node :k (if w2 [(kframe w2)] [])) rd])\n681\t        pending (second (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame))))\n682\t        parked (rows/node :act [park-address 3 env (rows/node :window []) (rows/node :k [(kframe parked-value)]) 0])]\n683\t    (rows/node :task (-> (:slots base) (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 5 bindings)\n684\t                         (assoc 11 (merge ft/worked-code {park-address park-vector}))\n685\t                         (assoc 8 (rows/node :parked-table (if parked-value [7 parked] [])))\n686\t                         (assoc 13 [(rows/node :wait [wait-act pending])]) (assoc 16 {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork})))))\n687\t\n688\t(defn gen-frame-task [seed]\n689\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n690\t        k (inc (.nextInt r 4))\n691\t        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"f\" %)) (range k))\n692\t        body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n693\t        pick (fn [] (gen-value r cells 2))\n694\t        wait-values (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 2)) pick))\n695\t        env-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n696\t        parked-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n697\t        used (reduce into #{} (map reached (remove nil? (concat wait-values [env-value parked-value]))))]\n698\t    (if (empty? used)\n699\t      (recur (inc seed))\n700\t      (frame-task (mapv #(vector % body) (filter used cells)) wait-values env-value parked-value))))\n701\t\n702\t(defn gen-child-task\n703\t  \"A parent (seed) with two install children: one random child task and the\n704\t   review counterexample as a child; the parent's own values stay random.\"\n705\t  [seed]\n706\t  (let [parent (gen-task seed)\n707\t        child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (gen-task (+ 100000 seed))) 3 :child))]\n708\t    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12 [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n709\t                                                (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))))\n710\t\n711\t(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n712\t  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n713\t  []\n714\t  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n715\t        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n716\t    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n717\t                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n718\t\n719\t(defn with-child\n720\t  \"`parent` with one install 'a holding `child` (slot 3 set to :child).\"\n721\t  [parent child]\n722\t  (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12\n723\t                          [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots child) 3 :child))])])))\n724\t\n725\t;; ------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics\n726\t\n727\t(defn permissive-roots\n728\t  \"For a refused state: run the procedure with D5 replaced by host-first entry,\n729\t   over `n` shuffles of every unordered container. More than one root is a\n730\t   witness that the state is not interchangeable; one root over n shuffles is\n731\t   not a proof.\"\n732\t  [task n]\n733\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. 7)]\n734\t    (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)]\n735\t      (->> (repeatedly n #(rename-and-shuffle task identity))\n736\t           (map (fn [t] (binding [*numbering-opts* (assoc *numbering-opts* :permissive true)]\n737\t                          (let [d (discovery-numbering t)\n738\t                                [id _] (emit-node* t (:numbering d) {} [])]\n739\t                            id))))\n740\t           distinct count))))\n741\t\n742\t(defn invariant-roots\n743\t  \"Distinct roots of a state over `n` renamings plus shuffles (1 = invariant\n744\t   under the samples).\"\n745\t  [task n]\n746\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. 5) cells (all-census-cells task)]\n747\t    (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)]\n748\t      (->> (repeatedly n #(rename-and-shuffle task (zipmap cells (map (fn [i] (keyword \"r\" (str \"z\" i))) (shuffle (range (count cells)))))))\n749\t           (map do-root)\n750\t           distinct count))))\n751\t\n752\t(defn report-fixture [name task]\n753\t  (let [d (discovery-numbering task)]\n754\t    (println name)\n755\t    (println \"  DO :\" (or (:refused d) (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))) \"| work\" (:work (:used d)) \"| search\" (or (:search (:used d)) 0))\n756\t    (println \"  DO root:\" (do-root task))\n757\t    (println \"  6a root:\" (safe-6a-root task))))\n758\t\n759\t(defn invariance-check\n760\t  \"Root of the state and of the state renamed and shuffled; decode of the\n761\t   carrier. Returns a map of outcomes.\"\n762\t  [task ^java.util.Random r]\n763\t  (let [cells (all-census-cells task)\n764\t        fresh (zipmap cells (map #(keyword \"other\" (str \"z\" %)) (shuffle (range (count cells)))))\n765\t        task2 (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)] (rename-and-shuffle task fresh))\n766\t        d1 (do-root task) d2 (do-root task2)]\n767\t    (cond\n768\t      (and (map? d1) (:refused d1)) {:refused (:refused d1)}\n769\t      (map? d1) {:resource-limit (:resource-limit d1)}\n770\t      :else\n771\t      (cond-> {:root d1 :invariant (= d1 d2)\n772\t               :used (:used (discovery-numbering task))}\n773\t        true (assoc :decode (let [e (error #(decode* (emit* task)))]\n774\t                              (cond (nil? e) :ok\n775\t                                    (= :resource-limit (:yin.k/kind e)) :sixa-recompute-limit\n776\t                                    :else e)))))))\n777\t\n778\t(defn search [label gen seeds]\n779\t  (let [r (java.util.Random. 11)\n780\t        stats (atom {:numbered 0 :refused 0 :resource-limit 0 :invariance-fail 0 :decode-ok 0 :decode-fail 0 :decode-6a-limit 0\n781\t                     :refused-witnessed-noninterchangeable 0 :refused-not-distinguished 0\n782\t                     :max-work 0 :max-search 0 :max-local-runs 0})]\n783\t    (doseq [seed seeds]\n784\t      (let [task (gen seed) o (invariance-check task r)]\n785\t        (cond\n786\t          (:refused o)\n787\t          (do (swap! stats update :refused inc)\n788\t              (if (> (permissive-roots task 12) 1)\n789\t                (swap! stats update :refused-witnessed-noninterchangeable inc)\n790\t                (swap! stats update :refused-not-distinguished inc)))\n791\t          (:resource-limit o)\n792\t          (do (swap! stats update :resource-limit inc) (println \"RESOURCE LIMIT\" label seed (:resource-limit o)))\n793\t          :else\n794\t          (do (swap! stats update :numbered inc)\n795\t              (swap! stats update :max-work max (:work (:used o) 0))\n796\t              (swap! stats update :max-search max (:search (:used o) 0))\n797\t              (swap! stats update :max-local-runs max (:local-runs (:used o) 0))\n798\t              (when-not (:invariant o) (swap! stats update :invariance-fail inc) (println \"INVARIANCE FAIL\" label seed))\n799\t              (case (:decode o)\n800\t                :ok (swap! stats update :decode-ok inc)\n801\t                :sixa-recompute-limit (swap! stats update :decode-6a-limit inc)\n802\t                (do (swap! stats update :decode-fail inc) (println \"DECODE FAIL\" label seed (:decode o))))))))\n803\t    (println label (count seeds) \"states:\" @stats)\n804\t    @stats))\n805\t\n806\t(defn -main []\n807\t  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n808\t        s (fn [es] [:row (rows/node :set es)])\n809\t        v (fn [cs] [:row (rows/node :vec (mapv #(vector :cursor-cell %) cs))])\n810\t        c (fn [x] [:cursor-cell x])\n811\t        task (fn [ids result] (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % body) ids) result))\n812\t        two-cycle (task [:a :b] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])]))\n813\t        three-cycle (task [:a :b :c] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :c]) (v [:c :a])]))\n814\t        path-abc (task [:a :b :c] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :c])]))\n815\t        loop-2cycle (task [:l :a :b] (s [(v [:l :l]) (v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])]))\n816\t        seven (rt/seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square)\n817\t        ;; codex round 2: a key-building local run reaches a conflict, the root run does not.\n818\t        local-d5-witness (task [:a :b] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])])]) (c :a)])])\n819\t        refused-list [[\"loop + 2-cycle\" loop-2cycle] [\"2-cycle\" two-cycle] [\"3-cycle\" three-cycle]\n820\t                      [\"seven-cell\" seven] [\"path #{[A B] [B C]}\" path-abc]]]\n821\t    (println \"== fixtures (D5 mode: no residue search)\")\n822\t    (report-fixture \"fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y (pinned fab223...)\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y))\n823\t    (report-fixture \"review counterexample 'a #{X Y}, 'b X\" (fixture-review-counterexample))\n824\t    (report-fixture \"symmetric pair #{X Y}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(c :x) (c :y)])))\n825\t    (report-fixture \"alias a: [x x y]\" (task [:x :y] (v [:x :x :y])))\n826\t    (report-fixture \"alias b: [x y y]\" (task [:x :y] (v [:x :y :y])))\n827\t    (report-fixture \"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :y])])))\n828\t    (report-fixture \"{X 1, Y 1}\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) [:lit 1] (c :y) [:lit 1]])]))\n829\t    (report-fixture \"codex A: #{[X X] [Y Z]}\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])])))\n830\t    (report-fixture \"codex A reversed\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:y :z]) (v [:x :x])])))\n831\t    (report-fixture \"codex B: {X X, Y Z}\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])]))\n832\t    (report-fixture \"codex B reversed\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :y) (c :z) (c :x) (c :x)])]))\n833\t    (report-fixture \"codex C: #{#{X Y} #{Z W}}\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])])))\n834\t    (report-fixture \"codex C renamed\" (task [:q :r :s :t] (s [(s [(c :s) (c :t)]) (s [(c :q) (c :r)])])))\n835\t    (report-fixture \"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])]))\n836\t    (report-fixture \"[#{X Y} X]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (c :x)])]))\n837\t    (report-fixture \"[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]\" (task [:x :y :z :w] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]) (c :x)])]))\n838\t    (report-fixture \"#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]} (equal keys would be unsound; keys differ)\"\n839\t                    (task [:x :y :p :q :r :s] (s [[:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])]\n840\t                                                   [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :p) (c :q)]) (s [(c :r) (c :s)])])]])))\n841\t    (report-fixture \"codex r2 witness [#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A] (speculative key conflict; root run numbers)\" local-d5-witness)\n842\t    (println \"  invariant roots over 24 renamings+shuffles:\" (invariant-roots local-d5-witness 24)\n843\t             \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* local-d5-witness))) :ok))\n844\t    (report-fixture \"loop + 2-cycle #{[L L] [A B] [B A]} (6a pinned 55ff16...)\" loop-2cycle)\n845\t    (report-fixture \"2-cycle #{[A B] [B A]}\" two-cycle)\n846\t    (report-fixture \"3-cycle #{[A B] [B C] [C A]}\" three-cycle)\n847\t    (report-fixture \"path #{[A B] [B C]}\" path-abc)\n848\t    (report-fixture \"seven-cell triangle+square\" seven)\n849\t    (let [ids (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"x\" %)) (range 10))]\n850\t      (report-fixture \"ten cells in a vec\" (rt/task-node (mapv (fn [id i] [id (rt/cursor-cell-body :s i)]) ids (range))\n851\t                                                         (v ids))))\n852\t    (println \"\\n== refused states: permissive roots over 24 shuffles (>1 = witnessed non-interchangeable)\")\n853\t    (doseq [[name t] refused-list]\n854\t      (println \" \" name \":\" (permissive-roots t 24) \"distinct root(s)\"))\n855\t    (println \"\\n== D6 residue search on the refused states: root, invariance over 24 renamings+shuffles, trials, decode\")\n856\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n857\t      (doseq [[name t] refused-list]\n858\t        (let [d (discovery-numbering t)]\n859\t          (println \" \" name \":\" (do-root t) \"| numbering\" (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))\n860\t                   \"| trials\" (:search (:used d)) \"| work\" (:work (:used d))\n861\t                   \"| distinct roots\" (invariant-roots t 24)\n862\t                   \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)\n863\t                   \"| 6a:\" (safe-6a-root t)))))\n864\t    (println \"\\n== D6 does not move a D1-D4 pin (same roots with residue search on)\")\n865\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n866\t      (doseq [[name t] [[\"fixture\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)] [\"review\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]\n867\t                        [\"codex A\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))]\n868\t                        [\"codex C\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))]\n869\t                        [\"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])])]\n870\t                        [\"witness\" local-d5-witness]]]\n871\t        (println \" \" name \":\" (do-root t))))\n872\t    (println \"\\n== install child refusal translates in decode (path prefixed by the root id, then [12 name ...])\")\n873\t    (let [parent (with-child (rt/task-node [] [:lit nil]) two-cycle)]\n874\t      (println \"  lift:\" (error #(emit* parent)))\n875\t      (println \"  decode of a carrier built with emit-numbered:\"\n876\t               (error #(decode* (rows/emit-numbered parent {}))))\n877\t      (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n878\t        (println \"  with D6, lift root:\" (do-root parent) \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* parent))) :ok))))\n879\t    (println \"\\n== budgets: {:work 1} and {:search 0} are resource refusals, never canonicality verdicts\")\n880\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:budget {:work 1}}]\n881\t      (println \"  loop+2-cycle {:work 1}:\" (error #(emit* loop-2cycle))))\n882\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true :budget {:search 1}}]\n883\t      (println \"  3-cycle D6 {:search 1}:\" (error #(emit* three-cycle))))\n884\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:budget {:work 1}}]\n885\t      (println \"  decode of a valid carrier under {:work 1} propagates the resource refusal:\"\n886\t               (error #(decode* (binding [*numbering-opts* {}] (emit* (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)))))))\n887\t    (println \"\\n== key bytes versus stream descriptor size (codex r2: 128 -> 65536 chars grew key bytes 3509 -> 592208)\")\n888\t    (doseq [n [128 65536]]\n889\t      (let [big (fn [i] (rows/node :cursor-cell-body [(rows/node :stream [:s (apply str (repeat n \"d\"))]) i]))\n890\t            t (rt/task-node [[:x (big 0)] [:y (big 0)] [:z (big 1)] [:w (big 1)]]\n891\t                            (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)]) (s [(c :x) (c :z)])]))]\n892\t        (doseq [mode [:label :rank]]\n893\t          (let [d (discovery-numbering t {:spell-body mode})]\n894\t            (println \"  descriptor\" n \"chars, body spelled by\" mode \": key bytes\" (:key-bytes (:used d))\n895\t                     \"| work\" (:work (:used d)) \"| local runs\" (:local-runs (:used d)) \"| root\" (do-root t))))))\n896\t    (println \"\\n== invariance under element reversal + renaming/shuffling\")\n897\t    (let [r (java.util.Random. 3)]\n898\t      (doseq [[name t] [[\"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :y])]))]\n899\t                        [\"{X 1, Y 1}\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) [:lit 1] (c :y) [:lit 1]])])]\n900\t                        [\"#{X Y}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(c :x) (c :y)]))]\n901\t                        [\"review counterexample\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]\n902\t                        [\"codex A\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))]\n903\t                        [\"codex B\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])])]\n904\t                        [\"codex C\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))]\n905\t                        [\"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])])]]]\n906\t        (let [rev (with-redefs [shuffle (comp vec reverse)] (rename-and-shuffle t identity))\n907\t              ren (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)] (rename-and-shuffle t (fn [x] (keyword \"q\" (clojure.core/name x)))))]\n908\t          (println name \"DO:\" (do-root t) \"| reversed equal:\" (= (do-root t) (do-root rev))\n909\t                   \"| renamed+shuffled equal:\" (= (do-root t) (do-root ren)) \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))))\n910\t    (println \"\\n== frame-bearing fixtures (act window, K frame window, environments, parked state)\")\n911\t    (let [t (frame-task [[:x body] [:y body]] [(c :x) (c :y)] (c :y) (s [(c :x) (c :y)]))]\n912\t      (report-fixture \"wait window x, K window y, env y, parked K window #{x y}\" t)\n913\t      (println \"  decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))\n914\t    (let [t (frame-task [[:x body] [:y body]] [(s [(c :x) (c :y)])] nil (c :y))]\n915\t      (report-fixture \"wait window #{x y}, parked K window y (parked table, slot 8, precedes frames, slot 13)\" t)\n916\t      (println \"  decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))\n917\t    (println \"\\n== install children numbered by discovery order\")\n918\t    (let [child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (fixture-review-counterexample)) 3 :child))\n919\t          parent (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (rt/task-node [[:x (rt/cursor-cell-body :root/s 1)]] (c :x))) 12\n920\t                                         [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n921\t                                          (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))\n922\t          wire (emit* parent)]\n923\t      (println \"  parent root:\" (:yin.k/root wire) \"| task rows:\" (count (filter #(= :task (second %)) (:yin.k/rows wire)))\n924\t               \"| child numbering:\" (into (sorted-map) (:numbering (discovery-numbering child)))\n925\t               \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* wire)) :ok)))\n926\t    (println \"\\n== counterexample search, D5 mode (refuse the residue)\")\n927\t    (search \"random values\" gen-task (range 2000))\n928\t    (search \"frame-bearing\" gen-frame-task (range 200))\n929\t    (search \"with children\" gen-child-task (range 100))\n930\t    (println \"\\n== counterexample search, D6 mode (residue search)\")\n931\t    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n932\t      (search \"random values\" gen-task (range 2000))\n933\t      (search \"frame-bearing\" gen-frame-task (range 200))\n934\t      (search \"with children\" gen-child-task (range 100)))))\n935\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c7b287b-b5f3-49ed-8e51-732a8bb9b9de","uuid":"7d1ab90d-8a30-4a7b-94a2-a272893890b7","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:47:41.986Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj","content":"(ns probe.discovery\n  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (note D1-D6, round 3 of\n   2026-10-12) over the 6a abstract task representation (yin.vm.ucf.rows).\n   Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/.\n\n   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n\n   Shape of the procedure (one algorithm, three uses):\n   - a RUN traverses an item (D1), numbers cursor cells on first meeting (D2),\n     keys every :set / :map element by a speculative local run and defers equal\n     keys to the run's worklist (D1), re-examines the worklist to a fixpoint\n     (D3), groups the surviving tied elements into components by shared\n     unnumbered cursor cells, encodes every component by a local run over its\n     component item and adopts the least encoding's components (D4). A\n     component holding two elements of one group is refused (D5) or, with\n     :residue-search, resolved by trials (D6): one local run per element of\n     the duplicated group with that element entered first, the least\n     spelling adopted.\n   - the root run assigns [:n k], k = 1, 2, ...; a LOCAL run assigns [:u j r]\n     (j from the enclosing run's local counter, r the body rank) and its\n     spelling is the key. A KEY run is speculative: it never refuses, and its\n     residue (ties or conflicts) is spelled [:tie K ...] in the key. A\n     COMPONENT run (and a D6 trial) must be complete, else D5.\n   - :work is charged per position visited or spelled and per literal byte\n     copied into a spelling; :search per D6 trial; exceeding either refuses\n     :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n\n(def synthetic-table\n  \"Rows that exist only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an ordered pair,\n   and a component as the ordered list of its (group-id item) pairs, where the\n   item is the group's one element or a synthetic :set of its elements.\"\n  {:entry [:V :V] :component [:pairs :D :V]})\n\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n          :else schema)))\n\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n\n(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n          (= i (count b)) 1\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n(defn by-key-bytes [f xs] (sort #(compare-bytes (f %1) (f %2)) xs))\n\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n\n(defn elem-item\n  \"The item an element is entered and keyed as: a :set element is its V; a\n   :map entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v], so that the\n   alias pattern across key and value is part of the key.\"\n  [kind e]\n  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n\n(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n\n(def default-budget\n  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes; :search now counts D6 trials.\"\n  (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes))\n\n(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category))\n      (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n                      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]})))))\n\n(defn- literal-bytes [x] (count (cbor/encode x)))\n\n(defn item-cells\n  \"Cursor cells occurring in slot (d x); census and install children excluded.\n   Memoized per procedure on the slot value.\"\n  [ctx d x]\n  (or (get @(:cells-memo ctx) [d x])\n      (let [acc (atom #{})]\n        (letfn [(slot [d x]\n                  (cond (ref? d) (node x)\n                        (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                        (= :C d) (swap! acc conj x)\n                        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n                                         :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second x))\n                                         :row (node (second x))\n                                         nil))\n                        :else nil))\n                (node [n]\n                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n                    (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (child-task? ctx n))\n                      (dorun (map slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n          (slot d x)\n          (swap! (:cells-memo ctx) assoc [d x] @acc)\n          @acc))))\n\n(defn body-label [body]\n  (let [[stream position] (:slots body)]\n    [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n\n(defn body-ranks\n  \"Cursor cell -> rank of its body label among the census's distinct body\n   labels in bytewise order. Rank order equals label order, so spelling the\n   rank instead of the label changes no comparison and no numbering; it keeps\n   key bytes independent of the descriptor size.\"\n  [bodies]\n  (let [labels (into {} (map (fn [[c b]] [c (body-label b)])) bodies)\n        ranks (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i l] [l i]))\n                    (by-key-bytes bytes-key (distinct (vals labels))))]\n    (into {} (map (fn [[c l]] [c (get ranks l)])) labels)))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ runs\n\n(def ^:dynamic *trace* false)\n\n(defn- new-run [ctx num tag next first-item]\n  {:ctx ctx :num (atom num) :tag tag :next (atom next) :first (atom first-item)\n   :groups (atom []) :gid (atom 0) :refused (atom nil)})\n\n(defn- meet!\n  \"D2. A cursor cell met for the first time receives the run's next number.\"\n  [run c]\n  (when-not (contains? @(:num run) c)\n    (let [k @(:next run)]\n      (swap! (:num run) assoc c [(:tag run) k])\n      (swap! (:next run) inc)\n      true)))\n\n(declare enter-node! enter-slot! unordered! local-run resolve! spell-slot spell-node)\n\n(defn- enter-slot! [run d x path]\n  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n  (cond (ref? d) (enter-node! run x path)\n        (refs? d) (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i n] (enter-node! run n (conj path i))) x))\n        (= :C d) (meet! run x)\n        (v? d) (when x (case (first x)\n                         :cursor-cell (meet! run (second x))\n                         :row (enter-node! run (second x) path)\n                         nil))\n        :else nil))\n\n(defn- enter-node!\n  \"D1. Canonical traversal: table order for slots; ordered repeated slots in\n   order; keyed structural rows in the Jing byte order of the key; :window in\n   ascending register order; installs and module stores by name; :set and\n   :map by local keys with ties deferred; census and children not entered.\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n      (child-task? ctx n) nil\n      (= :task kind)\n      (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n        (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n          (doseq [child (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first (:slots %))) x)]\n            (enter-node! run child (conj path i (first (:slots child)))))\n          (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (doseq [[k v] (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %)) (partition 2 slots))]\n        (enter-slot! run (second ds) v (conj path k)))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (doseq [[r v] (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))] (enter-slot! run :V v (conj path r)))\n      (= :set kind) (unordered! run :set (vec slots) path)\n      (= :map kind) (unordered! run :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)) path)\n      :else (dorun (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (enter-slot! run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n(defn- local-key [run item]\n  (let [r (local-run run item nil)] [(bytes-key (:spell r)) (:spell r)]))\n\n(defn- unordered!\n  \"D1 for an unordered container: key every element by a speculative local\n   run under the numbering at this moment; enter singletons in key order;\n   defer equal runs as one tie group (deferral order = canonical order). A D6\n   trial run enters its forced element first, when its container is keyed.\"\n  [run kind elems path]\n  (let [f @(:first run)\n        forced (when f (first (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (= f (elem-item kind e)) i)) elems)))]\n    (when forced\n      (reset! (:first run) nil)\n      (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind (nth elems forced)) (conj path forced)))\n    (let [keyed (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when (not= i forced) [(local-key run (elem-item kind e)) e i])) elems))\n          groups (partition-by (comp first first) (sort #(compare-bytes (first (first %1)) (first (first %2))) keyed))]\n      (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n        (println \"  [sort]\" path \"num\" @(:num run))\n        (doseq [g groups] (println \"    \" (if (= 1 (count g)) \"enter\" \"DEFER\") (mapv (fn [[[_ sp] e _]] [e sp]) g))))\n      (doseq [g groups]\n        (if (= 1 (count g))\n          (let [[_ e i] (first g)] (enter-slot! run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))\n          (swap! (:groups run) conj {:id (swap! (:gid run) inc) :kind kind :path path\n                                     :key (second (first (first g))) :elems (mapv second g)}))))))\n\n(defn- fixpoint!\n  \"D3. Re-examine every deferred group in deferral order under the current\n   numbering; repeat while a round numbers a cursor cell and groups remain.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (let [before (count @(:num run)) groups @(:groups run)]\n      (reset! (:groups run) [])\n      (doseq [{:keys [kind elems path]} groups] (unordered! run kind elems path))\n      (when (and (seq @(:groups run)) (> (count @(:num run)) before)) (recur)))))\n\n(defn- components\n  \"D4. Tied elements of all surviving groups, linked by shared unnumbered\n   cursor cells (union-find). A component is :conflict when it holds two\n   elements of one group; :dup-group is the least such group.\"\n  [run]\n  (let [num @(:num run) ctx (:ctx run)\n        by-id (into {} (map (juxt :id identity)) @(:groups run))\n        elems (vec (for [g @(:groups run) [i e] (map-indexed vector (:elems g))]\n                     {:group (:id g) :kind (:kind g) :elem e :idx i\n                      :cells (set (remove #(contains? num %) (item-cells ctx :V (elem-item (:kind g) e))))}))\n        parent (atom (vec (range (count elems))))\n        find (fn find [i] (let [p (@parent i)] (if (= p i) i (let [r (find p)] (swap! parent assoc i r) r))))\n        union (fn [i j] (let [ri (find i) rj (find j)] (when (not= ri rj) (swap! parent assoc ri rj))))\n        by-cell (reduce (fn [m [i el]] (reduce (fn [m c] (update m c (fnil conj []) i)) m (:cells el)))\n                        {} (map-indexed vector elems))]\n    (doseq [[_ is] by-cell] (doseq [j (rest is)] (union (first is) j)))\n    (->> (group-by find (range (count elems)))\n         (sort-by first)\n         (mapv (fn [[_ is]]\n                 (let [members (vec (sort-by (juxt :group :idx) (map elems is)))\n                       dup (first (sort (keep (fn [[g c]] (when (> c 1) g)) (frequencies (map :group members)))))]\n                   {:members members\n                    :conflict (some? dup)\n                    :dup-group dup\n                    :path (:path (get by-id (or dup (:group (first members)))))\n                    :cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat :cells members))))}))))))\n\n(defn- component-item\n  \"[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …] in group order; iᵢ is the group's one element\n   in the component, or a synthetic :set of its elements when there are\n   several (the D6 case).\"\n  [comp]\n  [:row {:kind :component\n         :slots (vec (mapcat (fn [ms]\n                               [(:group (first ms))\n                                (if (= 1 (count ms))\n                                  (elem-item (:kind (first ms)) (:elem (first ms)))\n                                  [:row {:kind :set :slots (mapv #(elem-item (:kind %) (:elem %)) ms)}])])\n                             (partition-by :group (:members comp))))}])\n\n(defn- component-encoding\n  \"D4 for a certified component: a local run over its item, required to be\n   complete. D5 for a conflicting component: refuse. D6 (:residue-search): one\n   trial per element of the duplicated group, that element entered first; the\n   least complete spelling is the encoding. :permissive (diagnostic only):\n   a single trial with the host-first element.\"\n  [run comp]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run) item (component-item comp)]\n    (cond\n      (not (:conflict comp)) (local-run run item nil)\n      (:permissive ctx)\n      (let [m (first (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp)))]\n        (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m))))\n      (:residue-search ctx)\n      (let [es (filter #(= (:dup-group comp) (:group %)) (:members comp))\n            trials (mapv (fn [m] (spend! ctx :search 1) (local-run run item (elem-item (:kind m) (:elem m)))) es)]\n        (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n          (doseq [t trials] (println \"  [trial]\" (:complete? t) (:spell t))))\n        (or (first (remove :complete? trials))\n            (first (sort-by #(bytes-key (:spell %)) compare-bytes trials))))\n      :else {:complete? false\n             :refused {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :path (:path comp) :cursor-cells (:cells comp)}})))\n\n(defn- adopt!\n  \"D4. Enter a component by adopting its local run: its fresh cursor cells\n   receive the enclosing run's next numbers in local-index order.\"\n  [run comp]\n  (let [fresh (->> (:num (:ce comp))\n                   (remove (fn [[c _]] (contains? @(:num run) c)))\n                   (sort-by (comp second val)))]\n    (doseq [[c _] fresh] (meet! run c))))\n\n(defn- remove-elements! [run comps]\n  (let [gone (set (for [c comps m (:members c)] [(:group m) (:idx m)]))]\n    (swap! (:groups run)\n           (fn [gs] (vec (keep (fn [g]\n                                 (let [es (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (gone [(:id g) i]) e)) (:elems g)))]\n                                   (when (seq es) (assoc g :elems es))))\n                               gs))))))\n\n(defn- resolve!\n  \"D3-D6 to completion. Leaves :refused set (D5) with the worklist as of the\n   last fixpoint, or the worklist empty.\"\n  [run]\n  (loop []\n    (fixpoint! run)\n    (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n      (println \"  [resolve] num\" @(:num run) \"groups\" (mapv (fn [g] [(:id g) (:path g) (mapv (fn [e] (if (= :cursor-cell (first e)) (second e) e)) (:elems g))]) @(:groups run))))\n    (when (seq @(:groups run))\n      (let [comps (mapv #(assoc % :ce (component-encoding run %)) (components run))\n            bad (seq (remove #(:complete? (:ce %)) comps))]\n        (if bad\n          ;; The verdict is invariant; the diagnostic names every conflicting\n          ;; component's cells (sorted) and the least conflicting group's path,\n          ;; so it does not depend on host order either.\n          (reset! (:refused run) {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                                  :path (:path (:refused (:ce (first (sort-by #(or (:dup-group %) Long/MAX_VALUE) bad)))))\n                                  :cursor-cells (vec (sort (distinct (mapcat #(:cursor-cells (:refused (:ce %))) bad))))})\n          (let [sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %1))) (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %2)))) comps)\n                least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce (first sorted))))\n                least-run (take-while #(= least (bytes-key (:spell (:ce %)))) sorted)]\n            (when (and *trace* (= :n (:tag run)))\n              (doseq [c sorted] (println \"  [component]\" (mapv (fn [m] [(:group m) (:cells m)]) (:members c)) \"CE\" (:spell (:ce c))))\n              (println \"  [adopt]\" (count least-run)))\n            (doseq [c least-run] (adopt! run c))\n            (remove-elements! run least-run)\n            (recur)))))))\n\n(defn- local-run\n  \"A nested run on one item from the enclosing run's numbering, tag :u. Its\n   result is a key: the spelling under its final numbering, surviving ties\n   spelled [:tie K ...]. `first-item`, when given (D6 trial), is entered\n   before its siblings. Memoized on (item, numbering restricted to the item's\n   cursor cells, local counter, first-item).\"\n  [run item first-item]\n  (let [ctx (:ctx run)\n        cells (item-cells ctx :V item)\n        num @(:num run)\n        start (if (= :u (:tag run)) @(:next run) 0)\n        mk [item (select-keys num cells) start first-item]]\n    (or (get @(:memo ctx) mk)\n        (let [sub (new-run ctx num :u start first-item)]\n          (enter-slot! sub :V item [])\n          (resolve! sub)\n          (let [spell (spell-slot sub :V item [])\n                r {:spell spell\n                   ;; Restricted to the item's own cursor cells: the result is a\n                   ;; function of the memo key, never of the enclosing context.\n                   :num (select-keys @(:num sub) cells)\n                   :complete? (and (nil? @(:refused sub)) (empty? @(:groups sub)))\n                   :refused @(:refused sub)}]\n            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :key-bytes (fnil + 0) (count (bytes-key spell)))\n            (swap! (:counter ctx) update :local-runs (fnil + 0) 1)\n            (swap! (:memo ctx) assoc mk r)\n            r)))))\n\n;; -------------------------------------------------------------- spelling\n\n(defn- spell-cell [run c]\n  (let [[t k] (get @(:num run) c) ctx (:ctx run)]\n    (case t\n      :n [:cursor-cell [:n k]]\n      :u [:cursor-cell [:u k (if (= :label (:spell-body ctx))\n                               (body-label (get (:bodies ctx) c))\n                               (get (:ranks ctx) c))]]\n      (throw (ex-info \"spelling an unnumbered cursor cell\" {:cursor-cell c})))))\n\n(defn- spell-slot [run d x path]\n  (spend! (:ctx run) :work 1)\n  (cond (ref? d) (spell-node run x path)\n        (refs? d) (vec (map-indexed (fn [i n] (spell-node run n (conj path i))) x))\n        (= :C d) (spell-cell run x)\n        (v? d) (if (nil? x) x\n                   (case (first x)\n                     :cursor-cell (spell-cell run (second x))\n                     :row [:row (spell-node run (second x) path)]\n                     (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes (second x))) x)))\n        :else (do (spend! (:ctx run) :work (literal-bytes x)) x)))\n\n(defn- spell-node\n  \"The content encoding CE of a row under the run's final numbering.\"\n  [run n path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ctx (:ctx run) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n    (cond\n      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n      (child-task? ctx n) [:child]\n      (= :task kind)\n      (into [kind]\n            (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]]\n                           (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n                             (vec (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (nth % 1))\n                                                (map #(spell-node run % (conj path i (first (:slots %)))) x)))\n                             (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))))\n                         (map vector ds slots)))\n      (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat identity (by-key-bytes #(bytes-key (first %))\n                                                  (map (fn [[k v]] [k (spell-slot run (second ds) v (conj path k))])\n                                                       (partition 2 slots)))))\n      (= :window kind)\n      (into [kind] (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (spell-slot run :V v (conj path r))]) (sort-by first (partition 2 slots))))\n      (contains? #{:set :map} kind)\n      (let [elems (if (= :set kind) (vec slots) (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n            tied (filter #(= path (:path %)) @(:groups run))\n            tied-elems (set (mapcat :elems tied))\n            plain (keep-indexed (fn [i e] (when-not (tied-elems e) (spell-slot run :V (elem-item kind e) (conj path i)))) elems)\n            ties (map #(into [:tie] (repeat (count (:elems %)) (:key %))) tied)]\n        (into [kind] (by-key-bytes bytes-key (concat plain ties))))\n      :else (into [kind] (map-indexed (fn [i [d x]] (spell-slot run d x (conj path i))) (map vector ds slots))))))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------- procedure\n\n(def ^:dynamic *numbering-opts*\n  \"Options for every run of the procedure in scope (root and install children):\n   :residue-search (D6), :permissive (diagnostic), :spell-body :label (the\n   round-2 spelling, for the byte measurement), :budget.\"\n  {})\n\n(defn discovery-numbering\n  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n} :used {...}} or {:refused {...}}.\n   Throws :resource-limit when the budget is exceeded, and a defect (never a\n   refusal) when the numbering is not total.\"\n  ([task] (discovery-numbering task *numbering-opts*))\n  ([task opts]\n   (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n         bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n         ctx {:task task\n              :bodies bodies\n              :ranks (body-ranks bodies)\n              :memo (atom {}) :cells-memo (atom {})\n              :budget (merge default-budget (:budget opts))\n              :counter (atom {})\n              :permissive (:permissive opts)\n              :residue-search (:residue-search opts)\n              :spell-body (:spell-body opts)}\n         run (new-run ctx {} :n 1 nil)]\n     (enter-node! run task [])\n     (resolve! run)\n     (if-let [r @(:refused run)]\n       {:refused r :used @(:counter ctx)}\n       (let [numbered (set (keys @(:num run))) declared (set (keys bodies))]\n         (when (seq @(:groups run)) (throw (ex-info \"residue after resolve\" {})))\n         (when (not= numbered declared)\n           (throw (ex-info \"numbering is not total\" {:missing (vec (remove numbered declared))\n                                                      :extra (vec (remove declared numbered))})))\n         {:numbering (into {} (map (fn [[c [_ k]]] [c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" k))])) @(:num run))\n          :used @(:counter ctx)})))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- emission\n;; 6a's emit-node with install children numbered by discovery order and a\n;; row path threaded for the child refusal.\n\n(def ^:private ordered-slots @#'rows/ordered-slots)\n(def ^:private check-literal-markers! @#'rows/check-literal-markers!)\n(def ^:private byte-compare @#'rows/byte-compare)\n\n(declare emit-node*)\n\n(defn- refuse-unresolved! [r path]\n  (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n                   :yin.k/path (into path (:path r)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells r)})))\n\n(defn- emit-slot* [d x numbering rows path]\n  (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n    (check-literal-markers! (if (v? d) (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x)))\n  (cond\n    (ref? d) (emit-node* x numbering rows path)\n    (refs? d) (reduce (fn [[out rs] [j n]]\n                        (let [p (conj path (if (= :install (:kind n)) (first (:slots n)) j))\n                              [id rs'] (emit-node* n numbering rs p)]\n                          [(conj out id) rs']))\n                      [[] rows] (map-indexed vector x))\n    (= d :C) [(get numbering x x) rows]\n    (v? d) (case (first x)\n             :row (let [[id rs] (emit-node* (second x) numbering rows path)] [[:row id] rs])\n             :cursor-cell [[:cursor-cell (get numbering (second x) (second x))] rows]\n             [x rows])\n    :else [x rows]))\n\n(defn- emit-node* [n numbering rows path]\n  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n\n        [slots rows] (reduce (fn [[out rs] [i d x]]\n                               (if (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x)))\n                                 ;; An install child: its own scope, numbered by the same procedure.\n                                 (let [r (discovery-numbering x)\n                                       _ (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) path))\n                                       [id child-rows] (emit-node* x (:numbering r) {} [])]\n                                   [(conj out id) (merge rs child-rows)])\n                                 (let [[x' rs'] (emit-slot* d x numbering rs (conj path i))] [(conj out x') rs'])))\n                             [[] rows] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots))\n        slots (if (= kind :task)\n                (-> slots\n                    (update 7 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %)))\n                    (update 12 #(vec (sort (fn [a b] (byte-compare (nth (get rows a) 2) (nth (get rows b) 2))) %))))\n                (ordered-slots kind slots))\n        body (into [kind] slots) id (jing/segment-key body)]\n    [id (assoc rows id (into [id] body))]))\n\n(defn emit*\n  \"Lift side: number by discovery order, emit; refuses :yin.k/non-portable\n   :unresolved-cursor-cells (D5) for the root or for any install child (path\n   [12 <name> …]); a budget refusal propagates as itself.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (when (:refused r) (refuse-unresolved! (:refused r) []))\n    (let [[id rows] (emit-node* task (:numbering r) {} [])]\n      {:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root id :yin.k/rows (vec (vals rows))})))\n\n(defn decode*\n  \"Receiver side: 6a's decode* with its own numbering check off (it still runs\n   6a's recompute, under 6a's budget), then the whole discovery recomputation\n   (root and install children) under one translation: only a lift-side\n   :yin.k/non-portable of kind :unresolved-cursor-cells becomes\n   :yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells with the path prefixed by the\n   carried root id; anything else (a resource refusal) propagates as itself.\n   In the real codec this check sits at carrier step 6, before\n   validate-frames; here it runs after 6a's decode* returns.\"\n  [wire]\n  (let [task (@#'rows/decode* wire (assoc rows/default-budget :check-numbering false))\n        root (:yin.k/root wire)\n        id (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n                (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d)) (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n                      (throw (ex-info \"unresolved cursor cells\"\n                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n                      (throw e)))))]\n    (when (not= id root)\n      (throw (ex-info \"noncanonical\" {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :noncanonical-cursor-cells\n                                      :yin.k/path [root]})))\n    task))\n\n(defn error [f]\n  (try (f) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-data e) {:throwable (str e)}))))\n\n(defn do-root\n  \"Root id, {:refused …} for D5, or {:resource-limit path} for a budget.\"\n  [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (emit* task))\n       (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e\n         (let [d (ex-data e)]\n           (case (:yin.k/kind d)\n             :unresolved-cursor-cells {:refused d}\n             :resource-limit {:resource-limit (:yin.k/path d)}\n             (throw e))))))\n\n(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n\n(defn rename-and-shuffle\n  \"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set, :map and the\n   census in listed order (shuffle is rebound by callers for determinism).\"\n  [n renaming]\n  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n                       v))\n          (bij [x] (get renaming x x))\n          (go [n]\n            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n              (case kind\n                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))\n                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n                                              :else x))\n                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n    (go n)))\n\n(defn all-census-cells\n  \"Source ids of every census in the tree (root and install children).\"\n  [task]\n  (let [acc (atom [])]\n    (letfn [(node [n] (when (map? n)\n                        (when (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (swap! acc into (take-nth 2 (:slots n))))\n                        (doseq [[d x] (map vector (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))]\n                          (cond (ref? d) (node x) (refs? d) (run! node x)\n                                (v? d) (when (and x (= :row (first x))) (node (second x)))))))]\n      (node task) (vec @acc))))\n\n(defn seeded-shuffle [^java.util.Random r]\n  (fn [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. ^java.util.Collection (vec xs))]\n             (java.util.Collections/shuffle a r)\n             (vec a))))\n\n(defn decoded\n  \"Portable decoded equality of an abstract value (check-abstract!'s rule):\n   sets as sets, maps as maps, cursor cells by identity.\"\n  [v]\n  (case (first v)\n    :lit [:lit (second v)]\n    :cursor-cell [:identity (second v)]\n    :row (let [n (second v) ss (:slots n)]\n           (case (:kind n)\n             :vec [:seq (mapv decoded ss)]\n             :set [:set (set (map decoded ss))]\n             :map [:map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(decoded k) (decoded x)])) (partition 2 ss))]\n             [:row n]))))\n\n(defn distinct-decoded [vs] (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [e] [(decoded e) e])) vs))))\n\n(defn gen-value\n  \"Random V over `cells`, depth-bounded. Shapes: literals, cursor cells,\n   vectors, sets, maps (cursor cells as keys too), sets of cursor-cell pairs\n   (edges, aliases such as [X X] included), sets of sets.\"\n  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n        cc (fn [] [:cursor-cell (pick cells)])\n        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 9 3))]\n    (case k\n      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n      1 (cc)\n      2 (cc)\n      3 (let [elems (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :vec elems)] [:lit (mapv second elems)]))\n      4 (let [elems (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (pr-str %1) (pr-str %2))) (map (fn [p] [(decoded (first p)) p]))\n                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)]))\n      (6 7) (let [edges (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector :row (rows/node :vec [(cc) (cc)]))))]\n              [:row (rows/node :set edges)])\n      8 (let [inner (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt r 2))\n                                                  #(let [es (distinct-decoded (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) cc))]\n                                                     [:row (rows/node :set es)])))]\n          [:row (rows/node :set inner)]))))\n\n(defn reached [v]\n  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n                     nil))]\n      (w v) @acc)))\n\n(defn gen-task\n  \"A :halted task: 1-6 cursor cells over two bodies (ties common), 1-3 store\n   bindings and the result slot hold random values.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 6))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body :s (if (zero? (.nextInt r 3)) 1 0))) cells)\n        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n\n(defn worked-act []\n  (first (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame)))))\n\n(def park-vector [[:closure 0 [] 2] [:halt 0] [:park 0] [:return 0]])\n(def park-address (jing/segment-key park-vector))\n\n(defn frame-task\n  \"A blocked task carrying the worked code plus a park segment. The wait's\n   act holds `wait-values` in its window register 1 and in one K frame's\n   window register 1; `env-value`, when given, is bound to e0 in the free\n   environment and in every environment; the parked state (P 7) is an act\n   at the park site whose K frame holds `parked-value` in register 1.\n   Decodes under 6a's validate-frames.\"\n  [census wait-values env-value parked-value]\n  (let [base (rt/task-node (conj (vec census) [:test/cursor (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]) nil)\n        [seg pc env window k rd] (:slots (worked-act))\n        [_ store-of] (:slots env)\n        bindings (rows/node :bindings (if env-value ['e0 env-value] []))\n        env (rows/node :env [bindings store-of])\n        kframe (fn [v] (rows/node :kframe [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 v]) rd]))\n        [w1 w2] wait-values\n        wait-act (rows/node :act [seg pc env (rows/node :window [1 w1]) (rows/node :k (if w2 [(kframe w2)] [])) rd])\n        pending (second (:slots (rows/decode (rows/frame->rows ft/worked-frame))))\n        parked (rows/node :act [park-address 3 env (rows/node :window []) (rows/node :k [(kframe parked-value)]) 0])]\n    (rows/node :task (-> (:slots base) (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 5 bindings)\n                         (assoc 11 (merge ft/worked-code {park-address park-vector}))\n                         (assoc 8 (rows/node :parked-table (if parked-value [7 parked] [])))\n                         (assoc 13 [(rows/node :wait [wait-act pending])]) (assoc 16 {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork})))))\n\n(defn gen-frame-task [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 4))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"f\" %)) (range k))\n        body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        pick (fn [] (gen-value r cells 2))\n        wait-values (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 2)) pick))\n        env-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        parked-value (when (zero? (.nextInt r 2)) (pick))\n        used (reduce into #{} (map reached (remove nil? (concat wait-values [env-value parked-value]))))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (frame-task (mapv #(vector % body) (filter used cells)) wait-values env-value parked-value))))\n\n(defn gen-child-task\n  \"A parent (seed) with two install children: one random child task and the\n   review counterexample as a child; the parent's own values stay random.\"\n  [seed]\n  (let [parent (gen-task seed)\n        child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (gen-task (+ 100000 seed))) 3 :child))]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12 [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n                                                (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))))\n\n(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n  []\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n\n(defn with-child\n  \"`parent` with one install 'a holding `child` (slot 3 set to :child).\"\n  [parent child]\n  (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots parent) 12\n                          [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots child) 3 :child))])])))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics\n\n(defn permissive-roots\n  \"For a refused state: run the procedure with D5 replaced by host-first entry,\n   over `n` shuffles of every unordered container. More than one root is a\n   witness that the state is not interchangeable; one root over n shuffles is\n   not a proof.\"\n  [task n]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. 7)]\n    (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)]\n      (->> (repeatedly n #(rename-and-shuffle task identity))\n           (map (fn [t] (binding [*numbering-opts* (assoc *numbering-opts* :permissive true)]\n                          (let [d (discovery-numbering t)\n                                [id _] (emit-node* t (:numbering d) {} [])]\n                            id))))\n           distinct count))))\n\n(defn invariant-roots\n  \"Distinct roots of a state over `n` renamings plus shuffles (1 = invariant\n   under the samples).\"\n  [task n]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. 5) cells (all-census-cells task)]\n    (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)]\n      (->> (repeatedly n #(rename-and-shuffle task (zipmap cells (map (fn [i] (keyword \"r\" (str \"z\" i))) (shuffle (range (count cells)))))))\n           (map do-root)\n           distinct count))))\n\n(defn report-fixture [name task]\n  (let [d (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (println name)\n    (println \"  DO :\" (or (:refused d) (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))) \"| work\" (:work (:used d)) \"| search\" (or (:search (:used d)) 0))\n    (println \"  DO root:\" (do-root task))\n    (println \"  6a root:\" (safe-6a-root task))))\n\n(defn invariance-check\n  \"Root of the state and of the state renamed and shuffled; decode of the\n   carrier. Returns a map of outcomes.\"\n  [task ^java.util.Random r]\n  (let [cells (all-census-cells task)\n        fresh (zipmap cells (map #(keyword \"other\" (str \"z\" %)) (shuffle (range (count cells)))))\n        task2 (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)] (rename-and-shuffle task fresh))\n        d1 (do-root task) d2 (do-root task2)]\n    (cond\n      (and (map? d1) (:refused d1)) {:refused (:refused d1)}\n      (map? d1) {:resource-limit (:resource-limit d1)}\n      :else\n      (cond-> {:root d1 :invariant (= d1 d2)\n               :used (:used (discovery-numbering task))}\n        true (assoc :decode (let [e (error #(decode* (emit* task)))]\n                              (cond (nil? e) :ok\n                                    (= :resource-limit (:yin.k/kind e)) :sixa-recompute-limit\n                                    :else e)))))))\n\n(defn search [label gen seeds]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. 11)\n        stats (atom {:numbered 0 :refused 0 :resource-limit 0 :invariance-fail 0 :decode-ok 0 :decode-fail 0 :decode-6a-limit 0\n                     :refused-witnessed-noninterchangeable 0 :refused-not-distinguished 0\n                     :max-work 0 :max-search 0 :max-local-runs 0})]\n    (doseq [seed seeds]\n      (let [task (gen seed) o (invariance-check task r)]\n        (cond\n          (:refused o)\n          (do (swap! stats update :refused inc)\n              (if (> (permissive-roots task 12) 1)\n                (swap! stats update :refused-witnessed-noninterchangeable inc)\n                (swap! stats update :refused-not-distinguished inc)))\n          (:resource-limit o)\n          (do (swap! stats update :resource-limit inc) (println \"RESOURCE LIMIT\" label seed (:resource-limit o)))\n          :else\n          (do (swap! stats update :numbered inc)\n              (swap! stats update :max-work max (:work (:used o) 0))\n              (swap! stats update :max-search max (:search (:used o) 0))\n              (swap! stats update :max-local-runs max (:local-runs (:used o) 0))\n              (when-not (:invariant o) (swap! stats update :invariance-fail inc) (println \"INVARIANCE FAIL\" label seed))\n              (case (:decode o)\n                :ok (swap! stats update :decode-ok inc)\n                :sixa-recompute-limit (swap! stats update :decode-6a-limit inc)\n                (do (swap! stats update :decode-fail inc) (println \"DECODE FAIL\" label seed (:decode o))))))))\n    (println label (count seeds) \"states:\" @stats)\n    @stats))\n\n(defn -main []\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        s (fn [es] [:row (rows/node :set es)])\n        v (fn [cs] [:row (rows/node :vec (mapv #(vector :cursor-cell %) cs))])\n        c (fn [x] [:cursor-cell x])\n        task (fn [ids result] (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % body) ids) result))\n        two-cycle (task [:a :b] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])]))\n        three-cycle (task [:a :b :c] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :c]) (v [:c :a])]))\n        path-abc (task [:a :b :c] (s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :c])]))\n        loop-2cycle (task [:l :a :b] (s [(v [:l :l]) (v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])]))\n        seven (rt/seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square)\n        ;; codex round 2: a key-building local run reaches a conflict, the root run does not.\n        local-d5-witness (task [:a :b] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(s [(v [:a :b]) (v [:b :a])])]) (c :a)])])\n        refused-list [[\"loop + 2-cycle\" loop-2cycle] [\"2-cycle\" two-cycle] [\"3-cycle\" three-cycle]\n                      [\"seven-cell\" seven] [\"path #{[A B] [B C]}\" path-abc]]]\n    (println \"== fixtures (D5 mode: no residue search)\")\n    (report-fixture \"fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y (pinned fab223...)\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y))\n    (report-fixture \"review counterexample 'a #{X Y}, 'b X\" (fixture-review-counterexample))\n    (report-fixture \"symmetric pair #{X Y}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(c :x) (c :y)])))\n    (report-fixture \"alias a: [x x y]\" (task [:x :y] (v [:x :x :y])))\n    (report-fixture \"alias b: [x y y]\" (task [:x :y] (v [:x :y :y])))\n    (report-fixture \"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :y])])))\n    (report-fixture \"{X 1, Y 1}\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) [:lit 1] (c :y) [:lit 1]])]))\n    (report-fixture \"codex A: #{[X X] [Y Z]}\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])])))\n    (report-fixture \"codex A reversed\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:y :z]) (v [:x :x])])))\n    (report-fixture \"codex B: {X X, Y Z}\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])]))\n    (report-fixture \"codex B reversed\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :y) (c :z) (c :x) (c :x)])]))\n    (report-fixture \"codex C: #{#{X Y} #{Z W}}\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])])))\n    (report-fixture \"codex C renamed\" (task [:q :r :s :t] (s [(s [(c :s) (c :t)]) (s [(c :q) (c :r)])])))\n    (report-fixture \"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])]))\n    (report-fixture \"[#{X Y} X]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (c :x)])]))\n    (report-fixture \"[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]\" (task [:x :y :z :w] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]) (c :x)])]))\n    (report-fixture \"#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]} (equal keys would be unsound; keys differ)\"\n                    (task [:x :y :p :q :r :s] (s [[:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])]\n                                                   [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :p) (c :q)]) (s [(c :r) (c :s)])])]])))\n    (report-fixture \"codex r2 witness [#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A] (speculative key conflict; root run numbers)\" local-d5-witness)\n    (println \"  invariant roots over 24 renamings+shuffles:\" (invariant-roots local-d5-witness 24)\n             \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* local-d5-witness))) :ok))\n    (report-fixture \"loop + 2-cycle #{[L L] [A B] [B A]} (6a pinned 55ff16...)\" loop-2cycle)\n    (report-fixture \"2-cycle #{[A B] [B A]}\" two-cycle)\n    (report-fixture \"3-cycle #{[A B] [B C] [C A]}\" three-cycle)\n    (report-fixture \"path #{[A B] [B C]}\" path-abc)\n    (report-fixture \"seven-cell triangle+square\" seven)\n    (let [ids (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"x\" %)) (range 10))]\n      (report-fixture \"ten cells in a vec\" (rt/task-node (mapv (fn [id i] [id (rt/cursor-cell-body :s i)]) ids (range))\n                                                         (v ids))))\n    (println \"\\n== refused states: permissive roots over 24 shuffles (>1 = witnessed non-interchangeable)\")\n    (doseq [[name t] refused-list]\n      (println \" \" name \":\" (permissive-roots t 24) \"distinct root(s)\"))\n    (println \"\\n== D6 residue search on the refused states: root, invariance over 24 renamings+shuffles, trials, decode\")\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n      (doseq [[name t] refused-list]\n        (let [d (discovery-numbering t)]\n          (println \" \" name \":\" (do-root t) \"| numbering\" (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))\n                   \"| trials\" (:search (:used d)) \"| work\" (:work (:used d))\n                   \"| distinct roots\" (invariant-roots t 24)\n                   \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)\n                   \"| 6a:\" (safe-6a-root t)))))\n    (println \"\\n== D6 does not move a D1-D4 pin (same roots with residue search on)\")\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n      (doseq [[name t] [[\"fixture\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)] [\"review\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]\n                        [\"codex A\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))]\n                        [\"codex C\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))]\n                        [\"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])])]\n                        [\"witness\" local-d5-witness]]]\n        (println \" \" name \":\" (do-root t))))\n    (println \"\\n== install child refusal translates in decode (path prefixed by the root id, then [12 name ...])\")\n    (let [parent (with-child (rt/task-node [] [:lit nil]) two-cycle)]\n      (println \"  lift:\" (error #(emit* parent)))\n      (println \"  decode of a carrier built with emit-numbered:\"\n               (error #(decode* (rows/emit-numbered parent {}))))\n      (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n        (println \"  with D6, lift root:\" (do-root parent) \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* parent))) :ok))))\n    (println \"\\n== budgets: {:work 1} and {:search 0} are resource refusals, never canonicality verdicts\")\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:budget {:work 1}}]\n      (println \"  loop+2-cycle {:work 1}:\" (error #(emit* loop-2cycle))))\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true :budget {:search 1}}]\n      (println \"  3-cycle D6 {:search 1}:\" (error #(emit* three-cycle))))\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:budget {:work 1}}]\n      (println \"  decode of a valid carrier under {:work 1} propagates the resource refusal:\"\n               (error #(decode* (binding [*numbering-opts* {}] (emit* (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)))))))\n    (println \"\\n== key bytes versus stream descriptor size (codex r2: 128 -> 65536 chars grew key bytes 3509 -> 592208)\")\n    (doseq [n [128 65536]]\n      (let [big (fn [i] (rows/node :cursor-cell-body [(rows/node :stream [:s (apply str (repeat n \"d\"))]) i]))\n            t (rt/task-node [[:x (big 0)] [:y (big 0)] [:z (big 1)] [:w (big 1)]]\n                            (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)]) (s [(c :x) (c :z)])]))]\n        (doseq [mode [:label :rank]]\n          (let [d (discovery-numbering t {:spell-body mode})]\n            (println \"  descriptor\" n \"chars, body spelled by\" mode \": key bytes\" (:key-bytes (:used d))\n                     \"| work\" (:work (:used d)) \"| local runs\" (:local-runs (:used d)) \"| root\" (do-root t))))))\n    (println \"\\n== invariance under element reversal + renaming/shuffling\")\n    (let [r (java.util.Random. 3)]\n      (doseq [[name t] [[\"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :y])]))]\n                        [\"{X 1, Y 1}\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) [:lit 1] (c :y) [:lit 1]])])]\n                        [\"#{X Y}\" (task [:x :y] (s [(c :x) (c :y)]))]\n                        [\"review counterexample\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]\n                        [\"codex A\" (task [:x :y :z] (s [(v [:x :x]) (v [:y :z])]))]\n                        [\"codex B\" (task [:x :y :z] [:row (rows/node :map [(c :x) (c :x) (c :y) (c :z)])])]\n                        [\"codex C\" (task [:x :y :z :w] (s [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :z) (c :w)])]))]\n                        [\"[#{X Y} #{X Y}]\" (task [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (s [(c :x) (c :y)])])])]]]\n        (let [rev (with-redefs [shuffle (comp vec reverse)] (rename-and-shuffle t identity))\n              ren (with-redefs [shuffle (seeded-shuffle r)] (rename-and-shuffle t (fn [x] (keyword \"q\" (clojure.core/name x)))))]\n          (println name \"DO:\" (do-root t) \"| reversed equal:\" (= (do-root t) (do-root rev))\n                   \"| renamed+shuffled equal:\" (= (do-root t) (do-root ren)) \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))))\n    (println \"\\n== frame-bearing fixtures (act window, K frame window, environments, parked state)\")\n    (let [t (frame-task [[:x body] [:y body]] [(c :x) (c :y)] (c :y) (s [(c :x) (c :y)]))]\n      (report-fixture \"wait window x, K window y, env y, parked K window #{x y}\" t)\n      (println \"  decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))\n    (let [t (frame-task [[:x body] [:y body]] [(s [(c :x) (c :y)])] nil (c :y))]\n      (report-fixture \"wait window #{x y}, parked K window y (parked table, slot 8, precedes frames, slot 13)\" t)\n      (println \"  decode:\" (or (error #(decode* (emit* t))) :ok)))\n    (println \"\\n== install children numbered by discovery order\")\n    (let [child (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (fixture-review-counterexample)) 3 :child))\n          parent (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots (rt/task-node [[:x (rt/cursor-cell-body :root/s 1)]] (c :x))) 12\n                                         [(rows/node :install ['a :running nil {} child])\n                                          (rows/node :install ['b :running nil {} child])]))\n          wire (emit* parent)]\n      (println \"  parent root:\" (:yin.k/root wire) \"| task rows:\" (count (filter #(= :task (second %)) (:yin.k/rows wire)))\n               \"| child numbering:\" (into (sorted-map) (:numbering (discovery-numbering child)))\n               \"| decode:\" (or (error #(decode* wire)) :ok)))\n    (println \"\\n== counterexample search, D5 mode (refuse the residue)\")\n    (search \"random values\" gen-task (range 2000))\n    (search \"frame-bearing\" gen-frame-task (range 200))\n    (search \"with children\" gen-child-task (range 100))\n    (println \"\\n== counterexample search, D6 mode (residue search)\")\n    (binding [*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}]\n      (search \"random values\" gen-task (range 2000))\n      (search \"frame-bearing\" gen-frame-task (range 200))\n      (search \"with children\" gen-child-task (range 100)))))\n","numLines":935,"startLine":1,"totalLines":935}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FQ2mN86soJ8Mvw3NhAu9Zo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019dcPnrPoUpxnjeVzrofT1g","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\n4\trefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n5\t(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\n6\tproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\n7\tis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n8\t**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\n9\tafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n10\t`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\n11\tRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n12\t(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\n13\tfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n14\t`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\n15\twork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\n16\tkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\n17\tsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n18\t3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\n19\twhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n20\t[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n21\t(the carrier, body version 3) and\n22\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\n23\tvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\n24\tcell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n25\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n26\t(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\n27\tcode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n28\t\n29\t**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\n30\tmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\n31\tthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\n32\talias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\n33\tdefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\n34\tlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\n35\tand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\n36\tholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\n37\tcannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\n38\tover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n39\t\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n40\t\n41\t## 0. Vocabulary used below\n42\t\n43\t- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n44\t  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n45\t  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n46\t  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n47\t  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n48\t  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n49\t  change the result.\n50\t- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n51\t  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n52\t- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n53\t  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n54\t  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n55\t- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n56\t  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n57\t  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n58\t  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n59\t  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n60\t  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n61\t  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n62\t    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n63\t    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n64\t    key as `[:tie …]`;\n65\t  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n66\t    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n67\t  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n68\t    element before its siblings.\n69\t- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n70\t  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n71\t  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n72\t\n73\t## 1. Findings\n74\t\n75\t**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n76\t(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\n77\tcursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n78\t\n79\t| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n80\t|---|---|---|\n81\t| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n82\t| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n83\t| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n84\t| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n85\t| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n86\t| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n87\t| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n88\t| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n89\t| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n90\t\n91\tSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n92\t`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\n93\texample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\n94\tcursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\n95\tenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\n96\tpending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n97\t\n98\t**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\n99\tstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\n100\tit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\n101\tfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\n102\tposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\n103\tnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\n104\texisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\n105\torder is a total order by construction.\n106\t\n107\t**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\n108\tcarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\n109\tcell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\n110\trepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\n111\telement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\n112\tthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n113\t`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\n114\tkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\n115\tspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\n116\trecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\n117\tre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\n118\tEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\n119\tre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\n120\tprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\n121\ta key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\n122\tof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n123\t\n124\t**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\n125\tfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\n126\tonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\n127\tunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n128\t**component**. Two cases:\n129\t- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n130\t  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n131\t  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n132\t  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n133\t  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n134\t  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n135\t  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n136\t  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n137\t- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n138\t  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n139\t  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n140\t  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n141\t  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n142\t  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n143\t  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n144\t  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n145\t  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n146\t  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n147\t  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n148\t  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n149\t  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n150\t  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n151\t  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n152\t  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n153\t  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n154\t  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n155\t  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n156\t\n157\t  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n158\t  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n159\t  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n160\t  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n161\t  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n162\t  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n163\t  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n164\t  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n165\t  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n166\t  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n167\t  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n168\t  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n169\t  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n170\t  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n171\t  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n172\t  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n173\t  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n174\t  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n175\t  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n176\t  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n177\t  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n178\t  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n179\t  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n180\t  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n181\t  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n182\t  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n183\t  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n184\t  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n185\t  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n186\t  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n187\t  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n188\t  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n189\t  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n190\t\n191\t**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\n192\trefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\n193\tof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\n194\tcensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n195\t\"Work\".\n196\t\n197\t**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\n198\tcursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\n199\tmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\n200\tsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\n201\tframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\n202\tstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\n203\trenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\n204\tfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\n205\trefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n206\t200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\n207\tand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\n208\tthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\n209\tof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\n210\tspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\n211\tdescriptor.\n212\t\n213\t**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n214\t\n215\t## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n216\t\n217\tInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\n218\tcells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\n219\ttask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\n220\trefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\n221\trule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n222\t\n223\t**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\n224\ttraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\n225\tnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\n226\trun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\n227\tenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\n228\tcell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\n229\tso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\n230\tand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\n231\tcell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\n232\tchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\n233\texpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n234\t`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n235\t`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\n236\tentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\n237\tan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\n238\tspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\n239\torder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\n240\telements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\n241\twhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n242\t`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\n243\tbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n244\t\n245\t**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\n246\trow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n247\t(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\n248\tkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n249\t`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n250\t§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\n251\tspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\n252\tnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n253\t**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\n254\tat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\n255\tone numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\n256\tequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\n257\torder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\n258\tcontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\n259\tan entry's key before its value.\n260\t\n261\t**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n262\t`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\n263\tcell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\n264\tcounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\n265\tlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\n266\tenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\n267\tno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n268\t`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\n269\tcontents table.\n270\t\n271\t**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\n272\tre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\n273\tthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\n274\tre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\n275\tcell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\n276\tit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\n277\tjoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\n278\tone worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\n279\tgroups from every depth.\n280\t\n281\t**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\n282\tlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\n283\tcell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\n284\tfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\n285\tso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\n286\tby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n287\t`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\n288\tsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\n289\tgroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\n290\tsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\n291\tcomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\n292\treaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\n293\tcomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\n294\teach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\n295\tin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\n296\tnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\n297\tlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\n298\tRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\n299\twhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\n300\tcarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\n301\thost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n302\t\n303\t**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\n304\tit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\n305\troot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\n306\trun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n307\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\n308\tholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n309\t`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\n310\tcursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\n311\tdiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\n312\tnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\n313\trefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\n314\ta resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n315\t\n316\t**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\n317\tconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\n318\tgroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n319\t`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\n320\trow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\n321\tits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\n322\tindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\n323\tcompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\n324\tcomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\n325\tcomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\n326\tisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\n327\tstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\n328\tresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\n329\tdefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\n330\ttrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n331\t\n332\t**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\n333\tBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\n334\tbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\n335\tdefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\n336\trefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n337\t\n338\t**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\n339\tlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\n340\trestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n341\t(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\n342\tmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\n343\tprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\n344\tanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\n345\tholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\n346\tunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\n347\tclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n348\t\n349\t**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\n350\tthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\n351\ton mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n352\t(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n353\t(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\n354\tonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n355\t(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\n356\tkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\n357\tcarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\n358\ta resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\n359\titself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\n360\talready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\n361\tinstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n362\t`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\n363\tprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\n364\tvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n365\t`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n366\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\n367\ttranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n368\t\n369\t**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\n370\tround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\n371\tdescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\n372\tthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\n373\tThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\n374\tthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\n375\tevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\n376\tbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\n377\tcopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\n378\tstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\n379\tcarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\n380\tresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n381\t[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\n382\twas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\n383\tso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\n384\tcarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\n385\ta key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\n386\tthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\n387\tbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\n388\twithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n389\t`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n390\t`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\n391\tcells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\n392\tthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\n393\tunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n394\t200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n395\t`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n396\t\n397\t## 3. Proof of canonicality\n398\t\n399\tCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n400\t**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\n401\tother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\n402\torder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n403\t(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\n404\tThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\n405\tunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\n406\ta key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\n407\tunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\n408\tunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\n409\tthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n410\t\n411\t**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\n412\tof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n413\t(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\n414\tnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\n415\tcontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n416\t(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\n417\tcovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\n418\tconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\n419\ta function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\n420\tcorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\n421\tof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\n422\tisomorphism.\n423\t\n424\t**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\n425\tconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n426\t\n427\t**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\n428\tof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n429\t`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\n430\tdisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\n431\tunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\n432\tEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\n433\tspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\n434\tindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\n435\telements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\n436\tbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\n437\tgroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n438\t`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n439\t`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\n440\tbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\n441\ttwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\n442\tagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\n443\tthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\n444\tgroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n445\t\n446\t**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\n447\tcomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\n448\tcomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\n449\tcells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\n450\tisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\n451\tand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\n452\tdown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\n453\tconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\n454\tA iii).\n455\t\n456\t**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\n457\telements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\n458\tconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\n459\tgroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\n460\tconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\n461\ton the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\n462\tenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\n463\tthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\n464\tspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\n465\twith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\n466\tcomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\n467\tisomorphic configurations. ∎\n468\t\n469\t**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\n470\tsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\n471\trows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\n472\tof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\n473\tdeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\n474\tcanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\n475\tand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n476\t(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\n477\tnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\n478\tunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\n479\tconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n480\t\n481\tConsequences:\n482\t\n483\t1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n484\t   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n485\t   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n486\t   point lemma C covers.\n487\t2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n488\t   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n489\t   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n490\t   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n491\t   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n492\t   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n493\t   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n494\t   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n495\t3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n496\t   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n497\t4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n498\t   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n499\t   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n500\t   position get two numbers.\n501\t5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n502\t   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n503\t   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n504\t6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n505\t   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n506\t   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n507\t\n508\t## 4. Amendments\n509\t\n510\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\n511\twith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\n512\tsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\n513\tdefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\n514\tas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\n515\tof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\n516\tsets, maps and the census (proof in\n517\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\n518\tminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\n519\ttraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\n520\tguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\n521\tfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\n522\tconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\n523\tnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\n524\tcan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\n525\tState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\n526\tCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n527\t`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n528\t\n529\t**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\n530\tsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n531\tthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\n532\tto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n533\tthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\n534\tinvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\n535\tcensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\n536\torder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\n537\tchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\n538\town enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\n539\tlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n540\tprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\n541\tany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\n542\tnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\n543\tD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n544\t\n545\t**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\n546\tare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\n547\tof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\n548\tused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\n549\tan emitted row\".\n550\t\n551\t**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n552\troot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n553\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\n554\tbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n555\t\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\n556\tin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\n557\trefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\n558\tand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n559\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n560\t\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n561\t`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n562\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n563\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\n564\tthese counts are unchanged.\n565\t\n566\t**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\n567\trefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\n568\tis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\n569\ton decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\n570\tand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\n571\ttraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\n572\ttrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\n573\tspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\n574\tback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\n575\tthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n576\t→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\n577\tclass]\".\n578\t\n579\t**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\n580\tcanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\n581\ttraversal)\".\n582\t\n583\t**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\n584\tprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n585\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n586\t\n587\t**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\n588\tinvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\n589\tan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\n590\tthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n591\t(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\n592\tin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n593\t**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\n594\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\n595\tbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\n596\tas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n597\t(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\n598\tparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n599\t(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\n600\tare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n601\t`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\n602\tstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\n603\tLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\n604\tderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\n605\tD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n606\t\n607\t**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\n608\tof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n609\t\n610\t**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\n611\tone lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n612\t\n613\t## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n614\t\n615\tThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\n616\tcreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\n617\tmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n618\t(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\n619\tcounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\n620\tas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\n621\texecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n622\t`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\n623\tsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\n624\tcounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\n625\tstructure:\n626\t\n627\t- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n628\t  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n629\t  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n630\t  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n631\t  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n632\t  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n633\t  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n634\t  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n635\t  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n636\t  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n637\t- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n638\t  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n639\t  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n640\t  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n641\t- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n642\t  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n643\t  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n644\t  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n645\t  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n646\t  relative to its state.\n647\t\n648\tDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\n649\tidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\n650\tn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\n651\tposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\n652\tids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\n653\tonly kind proposed.\n654\t\n655\t## 6. Prototype\n656\t\n657\t`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\n658\tscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\n659\tone schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\n660\ttrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\n661\tspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\n662\tmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\n663\tdescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\n664\tchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\n665\twith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\n666\trecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\n667\tswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n668\t`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\n669\ttrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\n670\tfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\n671\thelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\n672\tthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n673\t\n674\t```\n675\tjava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n676\t```\n677\t\n678\tResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n679\t6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n680\t\n681\t| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n682\t|---|---|---|---|\n683\t| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n684\t| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n685\t| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n686\t| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n687\t| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n688\t| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n689\t| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n690\t| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n691\t| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n692\t| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n693\t| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n694\t| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n695\t| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n696\t| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n697\t| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n698\t| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n699\t| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n700\t| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n701\t| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n702\t| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n703\t| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n704\t| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n705\t| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n706\t| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n707\t| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n708\t| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n709\t| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n710\t| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n711\t\n712\tHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\n713\tfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\n714\treview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\n715\tFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\n716\tkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n717\t(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n718\t`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n719\t`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\n720\tcounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\n721\tdeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\n722\tagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\n723\tand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\n724\tis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\n725\tentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\n726\tReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\n727\tresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\n728\tthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\n729\tcomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\n730\teither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\n731\tand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\n732\tequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\n733\tin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\n734\tmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n735\t`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\n736\tK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\n737\tinner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n738\t`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\n739\tothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\n740\tties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\n741\tholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\n742\tover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n743\t(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\n744\tmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n745\t`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\n746\tnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\n747\trows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\n748\tspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n749\t6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n750\t`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\n751\tthe least is adopted. The example state of\n752\t`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\n753\tthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\n754\tsame rows under both procedures.\n755\t\n756\tPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n757\t`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n758\t`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n759\t`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\n760\twith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n761\t`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\n762\tstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\n763\town roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n764\t\n765\t## 7. Recommendation and cost\n766\t\n767\t**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\n768\tnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\n769\tunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\n770\ttests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\n771\tsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\n772\tschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n773\t30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\n774\treuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n775\t`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\n776\tlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n777\t`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\n778\texcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\n779\tnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\n780\treview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\n781\tthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\n782\tWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n783\t(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\n784\tsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\n785\tover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n786\t11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\n787\tcanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\n788\tthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\n789\tand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n790\t`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n791\t\n792\t**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\n793\tplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\n794\tdocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\n795\tnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\n796\trefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\n797\tunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n798\t\n799\t**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\n800\treviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\n801\tstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\n802\tand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\n803\trecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n804\t\n805\t**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n806\t(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n807\t\n808\t## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n809\t\n810\tAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n811\t(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\n812\timplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n813\t\n814\t**rows.cljc, deleted.**"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c7b287b-b5f3-49ed-8e51-732a8bb9b9de","uuid":"f97c91e4-5d93-4879-bef5-256e56e19137","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:47:41.998Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\nproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\nis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\nafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\nRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\nfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\nwork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\nkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\nsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\nwhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\ncode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n\n**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\nmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\nthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\nalias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\ndefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\nlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\nand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\nholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\ncannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\nover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n\n## 0. Vocabulary used below\n\n- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n  change the result.\n- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n    key as `[:tie …]`;\n  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n    element before its siblings.\n- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n\n## 1. Findings\n\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n\nSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\nexample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\nfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\nposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\nnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\nexisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\norder is a total order by construction.\n\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\ncarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\ncell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\nrepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\nelement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\nthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\nkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\nrecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\nre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\nEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\nre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\nunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\nEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\nspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\nindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\nelements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\nbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\ngroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\nbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\ntwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\nagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\nthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\ngroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n\n**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\ncomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\ncomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\ncells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\nand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\ndown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\nconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\nA iii).\n\n**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\nelements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\ngroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\non the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\nenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\nthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\nspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\nwith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\ncomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\nisomorphic configurations. ∎\n\n**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\nsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\nrows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\nof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\ndeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\ncanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\nand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\nnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\nunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\nconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   point lemma C covers.\n2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   position get two numbers.\n5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n\n## 4. Amendments\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\nsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\ndefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\nas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\ntraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\nguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\nfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\nconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\nnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\ncan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\nState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\nCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\nchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\nown enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\nlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\nD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\nused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\nan emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\nbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\nin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\nrefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\nand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\nthese counts are unchanged.\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\ntrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\nspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\nback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\nthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\nclass]\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\ntraversal)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\nprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\ninvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\nan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\nbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\nas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\nparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\nare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\nstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\nLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\nderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\nD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\nof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\ncounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\nas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\nsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\ncounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\nstructure:\n\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n  relative to its state.\n\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\nids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\nonly kind proposed.\n\n## 6. Prototype\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\none schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\ntrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\nspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\nmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\ndescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\nrecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\nswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\ntrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\nfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\nhelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\nthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n\n```\njava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n\n| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n\nHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\nfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\nreview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\nFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\nkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\ndeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\nagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\nand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\nis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\nentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\nReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\nresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\nthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\ncomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\neither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\nand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\nequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\nin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\nmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\nK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\ninner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\nothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\nties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\nholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\nover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\nmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\nnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\nrows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\nspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\nthe least is adopted. The example state of\n`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\nthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\nsame rows under both procedures.\n\nPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\nwith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\nstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\nown roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n\n## 7. Recommendation and cost\n\n**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\nnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\ntests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\nsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\nschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\nreuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\nlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\nexcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\nnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\nreview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\nthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\nWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\nsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\nover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\ncanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\nthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\nand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n\n**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\nplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\ndocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\nnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\nrefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\nunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n\n**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\nreviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\nstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\nand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\nrecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n\n**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n\n## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n\nAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\nimplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.**","numLines":814,"startLine":1,"totalLines":981,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019dcPnrPoUpxnjeVzrofT1g","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
