{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T3Kpod35SJjY1kjcr2DAtx","type":"tool_result","content":"134:**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,\n135-cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell\", which defines all four words; this document\n136-uses them exactly as defined there). Before 2026-10-12 this document called\n137-the cursor cell a \"cell\" and spelled its tags `:cells`, `:cell-body`,\n138-`[:cell C]`, `:yin.k/c-<n>`; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps those\n139-spellings. A cursor cell has a task-local id `C`\n140-in the form `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n141-numbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n142-carries it: a cursor-cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n143-query, and two tasks' `:yin.k/cc-1` are unrelated.\n144-\n145-**The census.** A cursor cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n146-are not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n147-cursor cell commits to the cursor cell's identity but not to what the cursor cell holds. The\n148-root therefore carries a **cursor-cell census**, a content row\n149-\n150-```clojure\n151-[X :cursor-cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n152-[Bᵢ :cursor-cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n153-```\n154-\n155-that maps every cursor-cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n156-slot of the root, so the root id commits to every cursor cell's contents. A\n157-change to any cursor cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n158-the root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n159-authenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n160-list exactly the cursor cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n161-root and per install child.\n162-\n163-**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n164-\n165-- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cursor-cell iref in a value, a\n166-  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n167-  and a `:cursor-cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n168-  (data), so no cursor cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n169-  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n170-  validates, it does not trust construction.\n171-- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n172-  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n173-  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n174-  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n--\n181:  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n182-\n183-## 4. The value union\n184-\n185-Every position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n186-a two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n187-\n188-| `V` | Meaning |\n189-|---|---|\n190-| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n191-| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n192-| `[:cursor-cell C]` | a reference to the identity cursor cell `C` |\n193-\n194-The union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n195-literal keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n196-can never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n197-`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n198-is the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n199-\n200-**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n201-arms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n202-`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n203-(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n204-Rational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n205-symbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n206-values and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n207-retained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n208-are not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n209-kind `:byte-string`.\n210-\n211-**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n212-portability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\n213-retained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\n214-hashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n215-**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\n216-refuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\n217-would carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\n218-reference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n----\n2:134:**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c7b287b-b5f3-49ed-8e51-732a8bb9b9de","uuid":"737e0d75-42d2-4914-92d2-233dcee13a97","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:47:51.637Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"134:**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,\n135-cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell\", which defines all four words; this document\n136-uses them exactly as defined there). Before 2026-10-12 this document called\n137-the cursor cell a \"cell\" and spelled its tags `:cells`, `:cell-body`,\n138-`[:cell C]`, `:yin.k/c-<n>`; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps those\n139-spellings. A cursor cell has a task-local id `C`\n140-in the form `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n141-numbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n142-carries it: a cursor-cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n143-query, and two tasks' `:yin.k/cc-1` are unrelated.\n144-\n145-**The census.** A cursor cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n146-are not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n147-cursor cell commits to the cursor cell's identity but not to what the cursor cell holds. The\n148-root therefore carries a **cursor-cell census**, a content row\n149-\n150-```clojure\n151-[X :cursor-cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n152-[Bᵢ :cursor-cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n153-```\n154-\n155-that maps every cursor-cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n156-slot of the root, so the root id commits to every cursor cell's contents. A\n157-change to any cursor cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n158-the root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n159-authenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n160-list exactly the cursor cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n161-root and per install child.\n162-\n163-**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n164-\n165-- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cursor-cell iref in a value, a\n166-  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n167-  and a `:cursor-cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n168-  (data), so no cursor cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n169-  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n170-  validates, it does not trust construction.\n171-- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n172-  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n173-  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n174-  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n--\n181:  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n182-\n183-## 4. The value union\n184-\n185-Every position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n186-a two-element vector whose first