{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019JjTtsbH6GKSywAnWkRRap","type":"tool_result","content":"1356\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n1357\t\n1358\t**Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell.** The specification\n1359\tuses four words, each with exactly one meaning. This subsection is their\n1360\tdefinition; every other document defers to it (2026-10-12, owner decision).\n1361\t\n1362\t| Word | Meaning | Defined in code |\n1363\t|---|---|---|\n1364\t| **cursor** | The immutable value of `dao.stream`: a logical-stream identity plus a position in that stream. It has no identity of its own; two equal cursors are the same value. `dao.stream.md`: \"A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\" | `dao.stream` (`:dao.stream/cursor`) |\n1365\t| **cursor cell** | The VM's mutable box `{:stream-id id :cursor c}` held in the task's `:resources`, which `stream/next!` overwrites with the successor cursor on every successful read. It has identity: two cursor cells holding equal cursors are two different cells. It is the identity-bearing object the row carrier serializes (the census, the numbering, aliasing). | `yin.vm.engine` `cursor-cell?` |\n1366\t| **cursor-ref** | The sealed reference (`:cursor-ref`, sealed under the task's capability secret) that a guest program holds; it names a cursor cell. Binding one cursor-ref under two names makes both names the same cursor cell. | `yin.vm.engine` `issue-ref`, kind `:cursor-ref` |\n1367\t| **cell** | Only the general mutable heap box, `yin.vm.cell` (`cell/new`), referred to by a `:cell-ref`. It is unrelated to streams. | `yin.vm.cell` |\n1368\t\n1369\tTwo invariants follow, and the carrier relies on both. (1) *A cursor inside a\n1370\tcursor cell is plain data.* Together with the stream's portable descriptor it\n1371\tis all a receiver needs: the receiver attaches to the stream at that position\n1372\tand mints its own cursor cell, so the carrier never has to reconstruct the\n1373\tsender's identity beyond naming which references share one cursor cell.\n1374\t(2) *Sharing must survive transport.* Repeated cursor-refs to one cursor cell\n1375\tadvance together after transport, and two cursor cells at one position remain\n1376\ttwo independently advanceable cells. Before 2026-10-12 the row carrier called\n1377\tthe cursor cell a \"cell\"; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps its shipped\n1378\tspellings (`:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/cells`).\n1379\t\n1380\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor cell is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n1381\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n1382\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n1383\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n1384\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n1385\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n1386\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n1387\texactly this distinction: two cursor cells at one position would collapse, and\n1388\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n1389\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n1390\t\n1391\t```clojure\n1392\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n1393\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n1394\t;; values reference cursor cells, never positions:\n1395\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c7b287b-b5f3-49ed-8e51-732a8bb9b9de","uuid":"40004991-204e-4d31-8299-bde24b8ada76","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:48:21.924Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md","content":"with these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n\n**Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell.** The specification\nuses four words, each with exactly one meaning. This subsection is their\ndefinition; every other document defers to it (2026-10-12, owner decision).\n\n| Word | Meaning | Defined in code |\n|---|---|---|\n| **cursor** | The immutable value of `dao.stream`: a logical-stream identity plus a position in that stream. It has no identity of its own; two equal cursors are the same value. `dao.stream.md`: \"A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\" | `dao.stream` (`:dao.stream/cursor`) |\n| **cursor cell** | The VM's mutable box `{:stream-id id :cursor c}` held in the task's `:resources`, which `stream/next!` overwrites with the successor cursor on every successful read. It has identity: two cursor cells holding equal cursors are two different cells. It is the identity-bearing object the row carrier serializes (the census, the numbering, aliasing). | `yin.vm.engine` `cursor-cell?` |\n| **cursor-ref** | The sealed reference (`:cursor-ref`, sealed under the task's capability secret) that a guest program holds; it names a cursor cell. Binding one cursor-ref under two names makes both names the same cursor cell. | `yin.vm.engine` `issue-ref`, kind `:cursor-ref` |\n| **cell** | Only the general mutable heap box, `yin.vm.cell` (`cell/new`), referred to by a `:cell-ref`. It is unrelated to streams. | `yin.vm.cell` |\n\nTwo invariants follow, and the carrier relies on both. (1) *A cursor inside a\ncursor cell is plain data.* Together with the stream's portable descriptor it\nis all a receiver needs: the receiver attaches to the stream at that position\nand mints its own cursor cell, so the carrier never has to reconstruct the\nsender's identity beyond naming which references share one cursor cell.\n(2) *Sharing must survive transport.* Repeated cursor-refs to one cursor cell\nadvance together after transport, and two cursor cells at one position remain\ntwo independently advanceable cells. Before 2026-10-12 the row carrier called\nthe cursor cell a \"cell\"; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps its shipped\nspellings (`:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/cells`).\n\n**Cursor cells.** A cursor cell is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\ncursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\nadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\nadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\npolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\nwaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\nexactly this distinction: two cursor cells at one position would collapse, and\ntheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n;; values reference cursor cells, never positions:\n{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}","numLines":40,"startLine":1356,"totalLines":3333}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019JjTtsbH6GKSywAnWkRRap","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
